window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["gurugram-2025-solved"] = {"id": "gurugram-2025-solved", "title": "Gurugram 2025, solved version (Anukriti)", "fullTitle": "Gurugram University 2025 psychology entrance, the solved version by Anukriti", "sub": "60 Qs · a differently worded version of the 2025 exam · every answer re-checked · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "A music student learned 'raag malkaus' and then, a month later, learned 'raag kaafi'. Every time he tried to sing 'raag kaafi' he began singing 'raag malkaus'. This is known as", "options": {"a": "The method of loci", "b": "Forgetting curve", "c": "Proactive interference", "d": "Retroactive interference"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the student is showing proactive interference, because the raag he learnt first (malkaus) keeps intruding when he tries to recall the raag he learnt later (kaafi). Interference theory holds that we forget mainly because other memories compete with the one we want, not because traces simply fade with time; John McGeoch (1932) made this case against the old law of disuse. Interference works in two directions. In proactive interference, old learning works forwards and disrupts recall of newer learning. In retroactive interference, new learning works backwards and disrupts recall of older learning. A memory aid: PRO means forwards (old spoils new), RETRO means backwards (new spoils old). Benton Underwood (1957) showed how powerful the proactive kind is: people who had already learnt many lists in the laboratory forgot a fresh list far faster over 24 hours than people who had learnt none. Interference is strongest when the two sets of material are similar, which is why two raags with overlapping phrases clash. Another example: Riya changes her phone number and for weeks recites the old one when asked for the new one; that is proactive. Option c is right because the earlier raag is blocking the later one, the classic old-disrupts-new pattern. Exam trap: examiners reverse the two definitions, so always ask which memory is being lost. Here the newer raag kaafi is lost and the older raag is the culprit, so it is proactive.", "topic": "Proactive and retroactive interference", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Maslow's Need Hierarchy Model is broadly divided into which two categories of needs", "options": {"a": "Material needs and emotional needs", "b": "Cognitive needs and emotional needs", "c": "Deficiency needs and growth needs", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the needs in the hierarchy of Abraham Maslow (1943) fall into two broad categories, deficiency needs and growth needs. Maslow first set out five levels, from the bottom up: physiological needs (food, water, sleep), safety, belongingness and love, esteem, and self-actualisation (becoming all that one is capable of becoming). In Toward a Psychology of Being (1962) he sorted them into two kinds. Deficiency needs, or D-needs, are the four lower levels. They arise from a lack, like a hole that must be filled: the lack creates tension, and once the need is met it stops motivating. Growth needs, or being needs (B-needs), sit at the top, with self-actualisation as the main one. They do not come from a lack, and they grow stronger as they are satisfied: a person who paints for the joy of it wants to paint more, not less. Maslow later added self-transcendence at the very top, and extended versions of the pyramid count cognitive and aesthetic needs as growth needs too. Example: Arjun, newly arrived in Pune for work, first worries about rent, safety and making friends (D-needs); years later, settled and respected, he learns the sitar purely for the joy of mastering it (a B-need). Option c is right because deficiency versus growth (being) is Maslow's own two-way division of his hierarchy. Exam trap: option b looks plausible because Maslow did write about cognitive needs, but they are a kind of higher, growth need, not one of the two main categories; and \"all of the above\" fails because only c is Maslow's split.", "topic": "Maslow's deficiency and growth needs", "fig": "Abraham Maslow"}, {"q": "What is an ego defense mechanism?", "options": {"a": "Part of Ego state that prevents rational thinking", "b": "Part of unconscious that attempts to control unacceptable Id influences", "c": "Part of Ego state designed to reduce barriers", "d": "Part of Ego state that aids development"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: an ego defence mechanism is an unconscious process that keeps unacceptable id impulses under control, holding them out of awareness or disguising them so that the person is spared anxiety. Sigmund Freud (1894) first wrote about defence in \"The Neuro-Psychoses of Defence\", and his daughter Anna Freud (1936) catalogued the mechanisms in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence. The ego must balance the pleasure-seeking id, the moralising superego and reality; when a forbidden wish threatens to break through, the ego feels anxiety as a danger signal and responds with a defence. All defences share two features: they operate unconsciously, and they deny or distort reality. Repression, the basic defence, pushes the wish out of awareness. Others disguise it: projection (seeing one's own impulse in others), reaction formation (showing the opposite feeling), displacement (redirecting the impulse to a safer target), sublimation (channelling it into a socially valued activity), rationalisation, denial and regression. Example: Kabir is furious with his strict boss but cannot admit it even to himself; he comes home and snaps at his younger brother over nothing. The anger has been displaced, and Kabir honestly does not see the link. Option b is right because it is the only option that names the job of a defence, controlling unacceptable id impulses, and it places defences outside awareness. Strictly, defences are unconscious operations of the ego, so \"part of unconscious\" is loose wording, but its function is right. Exam trap: options a, c and d use the phrase \"ego state\", which sounds Freudian but is best known from the transactional analysis of Eric Berne (1961) (Parent, Adult, Child); none of them describes what a defence actually does.", "topic": "Ego defence mechanisms", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Learning by observing behaviour of others and noting consequences of that behaviour is known as", "options": {"a": "Vicarious learning", "b": "Vicious learning", "c": "Ventral learning", "d": "Vexed learning"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: learning by watching another person's behaviour and noting what happens to them is vicarious learning, also called observational learning or modelling. \"Vicarious\" means experienced through someone else. Albert Bandura (1965) showed that people can learn a behaviour without performing it and without being rewarded themselves. In the earlier Bobo doll study (Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross, 1961), nursery-school children who watched an adult punch and kick an inflatable doll later copied those very acts. In the 1965 study, children saw the model rewarded, punished or given no consequence. Those who saw the model punished imitated less, an effect called vicarious punishment, while seeing the model rewarded (vicarious reinforcement) kept imitation high. Yet when all the children were then offered treats for copying the model, the groups did about equally well. They had all learnt the behaviour; the consequences had changed performance, not learning. In Social Learning Theory (1977) Bandura named four processes: attention (noticing the model), retention (remembering what was done), reproduction (being able to do it) and motivation (having a reason to do it). Example: Meera sees a classmate scolded and given zero marks for copying in a test, and she decides never to copy herself, though she has never been caught. Option a is right because the stem describes both parts of vicarious learning: observing a model and noting the consequences the model receives. Exam trap: the other three options are sound-alike words starting with \"v\", placed to catch a student who half-remembers the term; only \"vicarious\" is a psychological term.", "topic": "Vicarious (observational) learning", "fig": "Albert Bandura"}, {"q": "According to Cannon-Bard theory:", "options": {"a": "The thalamus plays a central role in producing emotions", "b": "The cerebellum must give the go ahead for emotions", "c": "Activity in the occipital and parietal lobes happens simultaneously to produce emotions", "d": "The sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems work in concert"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: in the Cannon-Bard theory the thalamus plays the central role in producing emotion. William James (1884) and Carl Lange (1885) had argued that bodily change comes first and the feeling is our perception of it: we are afraid because we tremble. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked this view. He argued that animals whose viscera were cut off from the brain still showed emotion, that much the same arousal goes with fear, anger and even fever, that the viscera are too slow to produce feelings that arise within a second, and that injected adrenaline produces arousal without true emotion. His alternative, which Philip Bard (1928) extended through experiments on rage in cats, was that an emotional stimulus is relayed by the thalamus, which sends signals at the same moment in two directions: up to the cortex, producing the conscious feeling, and down to the body through the sympathetic nervous system, producing arousal. Feeling and bodily change therefore happen together and independently; neither causes the other. Example: Riya hears a loud crack on a dark road, and her fear and her pounding heart arrive at the same instant. Option a is right because the thalamus, as the centre that triggers feeling and arousal in parallel, is the heart of the theory. Exam trap: option c borrows the word \"simultaneously\" to catch students who remember only that the theory is about simultaneous events; the simultaneity is between felt emotion and bodily arousal, not between two cortical lobes. Key note: Bard's own lesion work pointed to the hypothalamus as the trigger for the bodily side of rage, and later research moved the emotional centre to the limbic system; but the theory as taught names the thalamus, so a stands.", "topic": "Cannon-Bard theory of emotion", "fig": "Walter Cannon and Philip Bard"}, {"q": "The tendency for distributed study or practice to yield better long-term retention than is achieved through massed study or practice is called?", "options": {"a": "Serial positioning effect", "b": "Spacing effect", "c": "Recency effect", "d": "Primacy effect"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the advantage of spreading study over several sessions instead of cramming it into one is called the spacing effect, also known as the distributed practice effect. Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885), testing himself on lists of nonsense syllables, first noticed that repetitions spread over several days gave better retention than the same number of repetitions packed into one sitting. The effect has since been found with words, facts, motor skills and classroom material, in children and adults; Nicholas Cepeda and colleagues (2006) pooled hundreds of experiments and confirmed that spaced practice beats massed practice for long-term recall. One likely reason is that each spaced session forces effortful retrieval of partly forgotten material, which strengthens it; another is that the material is encoded in more varied contexts. Example: Kabir and Meera each give six hours to the nervous system chapter. Kabir does all six the night before the test; Meera does one hour a day for six days. Both may score well the next morning, but a month later Meera remembers far more. Option b is right because the stem is the textbook definition of the spacing effect: distributed practice beats massed practice for long-term retention. Exam trap: the other three options all belong to the serial position effect, the finding that in a list the first items (primacy) and the last items (recency) are recalled best (Bennet Murdock, 1962). Primacy reflects extra rehearsal into long-term memory; recency reflects items still held in short-term memory, and Murray Glanzer and Anita Cunitz (1966) showed it vanishes after a 30-second distracting delay. These effects concern an item's position in a list, not how practice is timed.", "topic": "Spacing effect", "fig": "Hermann Ebbinghaus"}, {"q": "Two train lines, although parallel to each other, still appear to converge at the end. This is an example of which kind of monocular cues?", "options": {"a": "Linear perspective", "b": "Convergence", "c": "Aerial perspective", "d": "Motion parallax"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: parallel railway lines that seem to meet in the distance show linear perspective, a monocular depth cue. Depth cues are the pieces of information the brain uses to build a three-dimensional world from the flat images on the retina. Monocular cues work with one eye, and most are pictorial, which is why artists can use them to make a flat canvas look deep. In linear perspective, parallel lines appear to converge as they recede, because the gap between them takes up a smaller and smaller image on the retina. Other monocular cues are relative size (the smaller of two similar objects looks farther away), interposition (a near object hides part of a far one), texture gradient (texture looks finer and denser with distance; James Gibson, 1950), aerial or atmospheric perspective (distant objects look hazy and bluish), shading, height in the visual field and motion parallax. Binocular cues need both eyes: retinal disparity (each eye receives a slightly different view, the principle of the stereoscope of Charles Wheatstone, 1838) and convergence (the eyes turn inwards to look at something near). Example: standing on the platform at Howrah, Arjun sees the two rails narrow to a single point at the horizon, though he knows they stay the same distance apart all the way. Option a is right because converging parallel lines are the defining example of linear perspective. Exam trap: the stem itself says the lines \"converge\", which tempts students to pick \"convergence\"; but convergence is the inward turning of the two eyes, a binocular cue, so it cannot answer a question about monocular cues.", "topic": "Monocular depth cues", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Weber's Law, well established in perceptual research, states that:", "options": {"a": "A stimulus must be increased by equal value to be just noticeably different", "b": "A stimulus must be increased by twice its amount to be noticeably different", "c": "A stimulus must be increased by a constant fraction of its value to be just noticeably different", "d": "A stimulus must be increased by ten times as amount to be just noticeably different"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: Weber's law states that a stimulus must be increased by a constant fraction of its value to be just noticeably different. Ernst Weber (1834), a German physiologist, asked people to compare lifted weights and found that the smallest change they could detect, the just noticeable difference (JND) or difference threshold, was not a fixed amount. It grew in proportion to the starting weight. In symbols, delta I divided by I equals k, where I is the starting intensity, delta I the change needed for a JND, and k a constant called the Weber fraction. Each sense has its own fraction, and a smaller fraction means finer discrimination. The law holds well across the middle range of intensities but breaks down for very weak and very strong stimuli. Example: Meera is lighting diyas for Diwali. With 10 lit, one more makes the room visibly brighter. With 100 lit, one more makes no visible difference; she needs about 10 more, because the ratio (1 in 10) must stay the same. Gustav Fechner (1860), in Elements of Psychophysics, built on Weber's finding to state that sensation grows with the logarithm of stimulus intensity (Fechner's law), and Stanley Smith Stevens (1957) later proposed a power law, in which each sense has its own exponent. Option c is right because \"a constant fraction of its value\" is exactly Weber's statement. Exam trap: option a, an equal absolute increase, is the opposite of Weber's law, because it ignores the starting level; also keep Weber (the JND is a constant proportion) apart from Fechner (sensation follows a logarithmic scale) and Stevens (a power function).", "topic": "Weber's law", "fig": "Ernst Weber"}, {"q": "The ability to focus on multiple things at the same time is:", "options": {"a": "Sustained attention", "b": "Divided attention", "c": "Selective attention", "d": "Alternating attention"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the ability to attend to two or more things at the same time is divided attention. Attention is the selection of some information for further processing, and its capacity is limited. Daniel Kahneman (1973), in Attention and Effort, described attention as a limited pool of mental resources shared among tasks: two tasks can run together only if their combined demand fits within the pool. Dual-task studies show that performance on both tasks usually drops when they are done together, unless one has become automatic through practice or the two use different channels, such as one visual and one auditory. The clinical model of McKay Moore Sohlberg and Catherine Mateer (1987), widely used in neuropsychological rehabilitation, separates five levels: focused attention (responding to a stimulus at all), sustained attention or vigilance (keeping attention over time), selective attention (attending to one input while ignoring others, as in the cocktail party effect studied by Colin Cherry, 1953), alternating attention (shifting back and forth between tasks) and divided attention (handling two tasks at once). Example: Riya rides her scooter through Delhi traffic while chatting with the friend sitting behind her. Her attention is divided across both tasks, and when the traffic suddenly thickens, the chat is the first thing to suffer. Option b is right because doing several things simultaneously is the definition of divided attention. Exam trap: alternating attention also involves more than one task, but the person switches between them one at a time; divided attention means handling them at the very same moment.", "topic": "Divided attention", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to Jean Piaget's cognitive development model, at which stage children are able to conserve, reverse their thinking and classify objects in terms of their many characteristic?", "options": {"a": "Sensory motor stage", "b": "Preoperational stage", "c": "Concrete operational stage", "d": "Formal operational stage"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: conservation, reversible thinking and classifying objects by several features at once first appear in the concrete operational stage, from about 7 to 11 years. Jean Piaget (1936) held that children build knowledge through schemes that adapt by assimilation and accommodation, and he described four stages. In the sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2) the infant knows the world through action and achieves object permanence. In the preoperational stage (about 2 to 7) the child uses symbols and language but is egocentric and centres on one feature at a time, so fails conservation. In the concrete operational stage the child acquires operations, mental actions that can be reversed. This brings conservation (knowing that a quantity stays the same when only its shape or arrangement changes), reversibility (mentally undoing a change), decentration (attending to several features at once), classification, including class inclusion (there are more flowers than roses), and seriation (ordering sticks by length). The limit is that this logic works on concrete, real things, not abstract hypotheses; that comes in the formal operational stage (about 11 onwards). Example: water from a short, wide glass is poured into a tall, thin one. Five-year-old Arjun says the tall glass has more; eight-year-old Meera says it is the same water, because you could pour it back. Option c is right because conservation, reversibility and multiple classification are the hallmarks of concrete operations. Exam trap: the preoperational stage is the near-miss, because conservation tasks are usually taught under it, but there the child fails them; passing them marks the concrete operational stage.", "topic": "Piaget's concrete operational stage", "fig": "Jean Piaget"}, {"q": "Who among the following believed that development occurred as a maturational (or biological) process regardless of practice or training.", "options": {"a": "Sigmund Freud", "b": "Arnold Gesell", "c": "John Watson", "d": "Jean Piaget"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: Arnold Gesell (1925) held that development is a maturational, biological process that unfolds on its own timetable, largely regardless of practice or training. Gesell, a paediatrician and psychologist at Yale, observed and filmed thousands of infants and children and published age norms for motor, adaptive, language and personal-social behaviour, which became the Gesell Developmental Schedules. Maturation is growth that follows a genetic plan, so all children pass through the same sequence, though each at its own pace. His best-known evidence is the co-twin control study with Helen Thompson (1929). The two girls were identical twins, so heredity was held constant. Twin T was given daily practice in stair climbing for six weeks from the age of 46 weeks; twin C had no training until 53 weeks, yet after only about two weeks of practice she had caught up. Training before the body was ready had gained little. Gesell's advice to parents followed: watch for readiness instead of forcing skills. Example: Kabir's parents worry that he is not walking at ten months and plan daily walking drills. A Gesell-style answer is that he will walk when his nervous system and muscles are ready, and drills will hardly speed that up. Option b is right because Gesell is the textbook representative of the maturational view, in which nature sets the pace. Exam trap: Jean Piaget (1936) also used biological ideas such as adaptation, which makes him tempting, but he stressed the child's active interaction with the environment; John Watson (1913) is the environmentalist extreme, the very opposite of Gesell.", "topic": "Gesell's maturational theory", "fig": "Arnold Gesell"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about Erik Erikson's theory of development", "options": {"a": "Erikson is a neo-Piagetian", "b": "Erikson's approach is based on the idea that people are products of their sexual instincts", "c": "A significant factor of Erikson's theory is that it describes the entire life span", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: a defining feature of the theory of Erik Erikson (1950) is that it describes development across the entire life span. Erikson, trained as a psychoanalyst in Vienna, set out his psychosocial theory in Childhood and Society. He kept the idea of stages from Sigmund Freud (1905) but made two big changes. First, he shifted the focus from sexual instincts to social and cultural relationships and the growing ego, so his stages are psychosocial, not psychosexual. Second, he carried development beyond adolescence, through adulthood and into old age. He proposed eight stages, each built around a crisis between two poles: trust versus mistrust (infancy), autonomy versus shame and doubt (toddlerhood), initiative versus guilt (preschool years), industry versus inferiority (school age), identity versus role confusion (adolescence), intimacy versus isolation (young adulthood), generativity versus stagnation (middle adulthood) and integrity versus despair (late adulthood). A good resolution of each crisis yields a strength, such as hope in infancy and wisdom in old age. Example: Meera, aged 45, trains young nurses and feels her work will outlive her, which is generativity; her grandfather, aged 78, looks back on his life with acceptance, which is integrity. Option c is right because covering the whole life span, from birth to death, is what set Erikson's theory apart from Freud's and Piaget's, whose final stages begin in adolescence and add nothing for adulthood. Exam trap: Erikson was a neo-Freudian ego psychologist, not a neo-Piagetian; examiners also swap his \"psychosocial\" with Freud's \"psychosexual\".", "topic": "Erikson's psychosocial life-span theory", "fig": "Erik Erikson"}, {"q": "Which of the following is the correct order of Kohlberg's theory of self-socialization:", "options": {"a": "Gender consistency, gender stability, gender labeling", "b": "Gender stability, gender labeling, gender consistency", "c": "Gender labeling, gender stability, gender consistency", "d": "Gender labeling, gender consistency, gender stability"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: in the account of Lawrence Kohlberg (1966), children move from gender labelling to gender stability to gender consistency. Kohlberg offered a cognitive-developmental theory of gender. Its central claim is that understanding comes first: once a child grasps that she is a girl, she actively seeks out and copies what girls do, so she socialises herself. This reverses social learning theory, in which rewards and models shape gender behaviour before any understanding. Gender constancy, the full understanding that one's sex is permanent, develops in three steps. Gender labelling, or basic gender identity (about 2 to 3 years): the child can correctly say \"I am a girl\" and label others. Gender stability (about 3 to 5): she knows gender stays the same over time, so a boy grows up to be a man. Gender consistency (about 5 to 7): she knows gender stays the same across situations and appearances, so a boy in a frock or with long hair is still a boy. Ronald Slaby and Karin Frey (1975) supported the theory: children further along in constancy paid more attention to same-sex adults in a film. A criticism is that children prefer gender-typed toys by about age 2, well before constancy. Example: Riya, aged 3, says \"I am a girl\"; at 4 she says she will be an amma when she grows up; at 6 she insists she is still a girl even after her hair is cut short like her brother's. Option c is right because labelling, then stability, then consistency is the order Kohlberg proposed, each step building on the one before. Exam trap: the options swap stability and consistency; stability (over time) always comes before consistency (across situations), and gender schema theory (Sandra Bem, 1981) is a different, later theory.", "topic": "Kohlberg's gender constancy stages", "fig": "Lawrence Kohlberg"}, {"q": "According to the preconventional morality phase in Kohlberg's theory:", "options": {"a": "Right and wrong are defined by the hedonistic consequences of a given action", "b": "It is based on social rules", "c": "Moral rules are seen as convention, designed to ensure greater good", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: at the preconventional level, right and wrong are judged by the hedonistic consequences of an action, the reward or punishment it brings to oneself. Lawrence Kohlberg (1958) studied moral reasoning by presenting dilemmas, the best known being the Heinz dilemma, in which a poor man must decide whether to steal an expensive drug to save his dying wife. He scored the reasons people gave, not the choice they made, and found three levels with two stages each. At the preconventional level, typical of children under about 9, morality is controlled from outside. Stage 1, the punishment and obedience orientation, calls an act wrong if it gets punished. Stage 2, the instrumental or individualism and exchange orientation, calls right whatever serves one's own needs, including fair deals of the \"I help you, you help me\" kind. At the conventional level, rules are followed to win others' approval (stage 3, good boy and good girl) and to maintain law and social order (stage 4). At the postconventional level, laws are seen as social contracts that can be changed for the greater good (stage 5), and finally as resting on universal ethical principles (stage 6). Example: asked why Heinz should not steal, Riya, aged 6, says \"because the police will catch him\"; asked why she helps her brother with homework, she says \"so he gives me his chocolate\". Both answers rest on consequences to herself. Option a is right because \"hedonistic consequences\", gaining pleasure and avoiding pain, is exactly the preconventional basis of judgement. Exam trap: option b describes the conventional level and option c the postconventional level; examiners shuffle the levels, so remember that \"pre\" means before society's conventions.", "topic": "Kohlberg's preconventional morality", "fig": "Lawrence Kohlberg"}, {"q": "Contact comfort is more essential in bond formation than providing for physical needs was concluded by:", "options": {"a": "John Bowlby", "b": "Jerome Kagan", "c": "Mary Ainsworth", "d": "Harry Harlow"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: Harry Harlow (1958) concluded that contact comfort matters more for forming an attachment bond than meeting physical needs such as feeding. Before his work, both psychoanalysis and behaviourism held a \"cupboard love\" view: infants become attached to the mother because she feeds them and so reduces their hunger drive. Harlow tested this at the University of Wisconsin with infant rhesus monkeys separated from their mothers soon after birth. Each was raised with two surrogate mothers, one made of bare wire mesh and one covered in soft terry cloth. For some monkeys only the wire mother held a milk bottle. These monkeys went to the wire mother to feed but spent most of their time clinging to the cloth mother. When a frightening mechanical toy was put into the cage, they ran to the cloth mother and, once calmed, used her as a safe base from which to explore. Harlow reported this in \"The Nature of Love\". Later work showed that monkeys reared with surrogates alone grew up socially and sexually abnormal, so contact comfort is needed but not sufficient. Example: baby Kabir takes his bottle from a nanny, but when a firecracker bursts outside he reaches for his mother, who holds and rocks him every evening. Option d is right because Harlow's surrogate mother experiments are the classic evidence that comfort, not food, drives attachment. Exam trap: John Bowlby (1969) also rejected cupboard love and cited Harlow's monkeys, so he is the near-miss; Bowlby built attachment theory, but Harlow ran the contact comfort studies. Key note: the printed key table in Anukriti's solved copy gives a (John Bowlby), but her own worked solution names Harry Harlow, and the official Gurugram key for the same question is Harlow; the table letter is a slip, so d stands.", "topic": "Harlow and contact comfort", "fig": "Harry Harlow"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a part of fore brain", "options": {"a": "Cerebrum", "b": "Cerebellum", "c": "Thalamus", "d": "Limbic system"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the cerebellum is not part of the forebrain; it belongs to the hindbrain. In the embryo the front of the neural tube swells into three primary regions, which give the brain its three main divisions. The forebrain (prosencephalon) is the largest. It divides into the telencephalon, which forms the cerebrum (the two cerebral hemispheres with their cortex), the basal ganglia and most limbic structures, and the diencephalon, which forms the thalamus and hypothalamus. The midbrain (mesencephalon) contains the tectum, with the superior colliculi for vision and the inferior colliculi for hearing, and the tegmentum, which includes the substantia nigra. The hindbrain (rhombencephalon) divides into the metencephalon, which forms the pons and the cerebellum, and the myelencephalon, which forms the medulla oblongata. The cerebellum, the \"little brain\" tucked behind the brainstem, coordinates balance, posture, the timing of movements and motor learning. Damage to it causes ataxia, an unsteady, drunken-looking walk and clumsy reaching. Example: Arjun's uncle has a small stroke in the cerebellum; his thinking and memory are intact, but he sways when he walks and misses the glass when he reaches for it. Option b is right because the cerebellum develops from the hindbrain, along with the pons and medulla. Exam trap: \"cerebrum\" and \"cerebellum\" look alike, but the cerebrum is the crowning part of the forebrain while the cerebellum is hindbrain; also remember that the thalamus, though it sits deep in the middle of the brain, is forebrain (diencephalon).", "topic": "Divisions of the brain", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What is the function of reticular formation", "options": {"a": "Arousal, alertness and attention", "b": "Refined motor movements", "c": "Emotions", "d": "Hunger"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the reticular formation controls arousal, alertness and attention. It is a net-like web of neurons (\"reticular\" means net-like) running through the core of the brainstem, from the medulla through the pons to the midbrain. Its ascending part, the reticular activating system, sends fibres up through the thalamus to the whole cortex and keeps it awake and responsive. It also acts as a filter, letting important or novel stimuli through and screening out routine background input. Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun (1949) showed its role: electrically stimulating the brainstem reticular formation in cats switched the EEG from the slow waves of drowsiness to the fast, low-voltage pattern of alertness. Damage to it can cause lasting coma. Its descending part helps regulate muscle tone, posture and some autonomic functions, but arousal and attention is its textbook role. Example: Meera sleeps through the steady hum of the ceiling fan, yet wakes at once when her baby whimpers, because her reticular formation lets the meaningful sound through and rouses the cortex. Option a is right because arousal, alertness and the filtering of attention are the classic functions of the reticular activating system. Exam trap: each wrong option names the function of a nearby structure: refined movement belongs to the cerebellum, emotion to the limbic system, and hunger to the hypothalamus.", "topic": "Reticular formation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following forms a part of the limbic system", "options": {"a": "Septal nuclei", "b": "Amygdala", "c": "Hippocampus", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the septal nuclei, the amygdala and the hippocampus are all parts of the limbic system. The limbic system is a ring of interconnected structures on the inner border of each cerebral hemisphere, curving around the top of the brainstem (\"limbus\" is Latin for border). James Papez (1937) proposed a circuit for emotion, and Paul MacLean (1952) named the wider network the limbic system. Its main parts are the hippocampus, the amygdala, the septal nuclei, the cingulate gyrus, the fornix and the mammillary bodies, with the hypothalamus and parts of the thalamus often included. The hippocampus consolidates new memories: after Henry Molaison (patient H.M.) had large parts of both hippocampi and the nearby medial temporal tissue removed in 1953, he could no longer form new lasting memories of facts and events. The amygdala detects threat and gives memories their emotional weight; removing the temporal lobes, amygdala included, in monkeys produced the tameness and loss of fear described by Heinrich Kluver and Paul Bucy (1939). The septal area is linked with pleasure and reward: James Olds and Peter Milner (1954) found that rats would press a lever again and again to stimulate electrodes placed there. Example: Kabir freezes when he sees a dog like the one that once bit him (amygdala) and can recall exactly where the bite happened (hippocampus). Option d is right because all three named structures are core limbic components. Exam trap: in an \"all of the above\" item, students often stop at the first famous structure, the amygdala or hippocampus, and miss that the less familiar septal nuclei also belong to the limbic system.", "topic": "Limbic system structures", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which lobes is responsible for vision", "options": {"a": "Frontal lobe", "b": "Occipital lobe", "c": "Parietal lobe", "d": "Temporal lobe"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the occipital lobe, at the back of the brain, is responsible for vision. Each cerebral hemisphere has four lobes, marked off by landmarks such as the central sulcus and the lateral fissure. The occipital lobe holds the primary visual cortex (V1), also called the striate cortex or area 17 on the map of Korbinian Brodmann (1909), which lies along the calcarine sulcus on the inner surface of the lobe. Signals travel from the retina along the optic nerve and optic tract to the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus, and from there to V1. The left occipital lobe receives the right half of the visual field and the right lobe the left half. From V1, information flows to surrounding visual association areas and then along two streams: a ventral \"what\" stream into the temporal lobe for recognising objects and faces, and a dorsal \"where\" stream into the parietal lobe for location and action. Damage to V1 causes cortical blindness or loss of part of the visual field, even when the eyes are healthy. Example: after a fall that injures the back of her head, Riya's grandmother cannot see anything to her left, though her eye doctor finds both eyes normal; the damage is in her right occipital lobe. Option b is right because the primary visual cortex lies in the occipital lobe. Exam trap: the temporal lobe (hearing, language comprehension, face recognition) and the parietal lobe (touch, spatial attention) both take part in later stages of vision, which makes them tempting, but the primary visual area is occipital.", "topic": "Occipital lobe and vision", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The part of the brain which connects the left hemisphere with the right hemisphere is called:", "options": {"a": "Corpus callosum", "b": "Inferior colliculi", "c": "Wernicke's area", "d": "Broca's area"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the corpus callosum is the structure that connects the left and right cerebral hemispheres. It is a thick, arched band of white matter lying deep in the groove between the hemispheres, made of roughly 200 million axons. A bundle that links the two sides of the brain is called a commissure, and the corpus callosum is by far the largest; the much smaller anterior commissure also joins the hemispheres. Through it the two halves share information, so that what one side sees or feels the other soon knows. In severe epilepsy, surgeons sometimes cut it to stop seizures spreading from one hemisphere to the other. Studying these split-brain patients, Roger Sperry (1968), who won the Nobel Prize in 1981, and Michael Gazzaniga (1967) showed that each hemisphere has its own specialities. When a word was flashed to the right hemisphere only, patients could not name it, because speech is usually controlled by the left hemisphere, yet their left hand could pick out the matching object. Example: Arjun has had split-brain surgery. Holding a comb in his left hand out of sight, he cannot say what it is, but with the same hand he can pick out a comb from other objects. Option a is right because the corpus callosum is the main commissure joining the two hemispheres. Exam trap: Broca's area and Wernicke's area are both language areas within one hemisphere, usually the left, and the inferior colliculi lie in the midbrain; none of them connects the hemispheres.", "topic": "Corpus callosum", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The function of myelin sheath is to:", "options": {"a": "Insulate nerve fibers from one another", "b": "Carry neural message", "c": "Act as energy center", "d": "Receive incoming information"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the myelin sheath insulates nerve fibres (axons) from one another and from the fluid around them. Myelin is a fatty, whitish covering wrapped in many tight layers around an axon, made by oligodendrocytes in the central nervous system and by Schwann cells in the peripheral nervous system. The sheath is not continuous: between the wrapped segments lie short bare gaps, the nodes of Ranvier, where sodium channels cluster. Because charge cannot leak out through the insulated stretches, current spreads quickly inside the axon to the next node, and the action potential is renewed only at the nodes. The impulse seems to leap from node to node: saltatory conduction (Latin saltare, to jump). The pay-off is speed: thin unmyelinated fibres conduct at about 1 metre per second, large myelinated ones at up to about 120 metres per second. Example: Meera, 28, develops blurred vision and tingling legs, and an MRI shows patches of lost myelin. This is multiple sclerosis, in which the immune system attacks central myelin, so impulses slow or fail. Option a is right because insulation is what the sheath itself physically does, and it is the means by which myelin speeds conduction. Exam trap: because the sheath wraps the axon, students pick 'carry neural message'; but the axon carries the impulse, and myelin only insulates it. Key note: the main purpose of myelin is faster conduction, and some papers key 'increases the speed of the impulse' instead; myelin gets that speed by insulating the axon, and among these four options only a names what myelin itself does, so a stands.", "topic": "Myelin sheath function", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Neural conduction within a neuron is:", "options": {"a": "Electrical process", "b": "Chemical process", "c": "Both a. and b.", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: conduction within a neuron is an electrical process. Communication in the nervous system has two legs. The first runs inside a single cell: signals picked up by the dendrites and cell body are summed at the axon hillock, and if the membrane there reaches threshold (about -55 millivolts) the neuron fires an action potential. Sodium channels open and positive sodium ions pour in, then potassium ions flow out to restore the charge. This wave of voltage change sweeps along the axon to its terminals without losing strength, and it obeys the all-or-none law. Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) worked out these ionic steps by recording from the giant axon of the squid. The second leg runs between cells. At the terminal the voltage change triggers the release of a chemical neurotransmitter, which drifts across the synaptic cleft and acts on receptors of the next neuron. Otto Loewi (1921) demonstrated this chemical step: fluid from a stimulated frog heart slowed a second heart, so a substance (his 'Vagusstoff', later identified as acetylcholine) must carry the message. Hence the classroom summary: electrical within, chemical between. Example: when Kabir steps on a thorn, the signal races along his sensory axon as an electrical impulse and then crosses to a spinal cord neuron chemically. Option a is right because the stem says 'within a neuron', the electrical leg. Exam trap: 'Both a and b' tempts students who remember the word electrochemical; in that summary the word covers the whole chain, synapse included. Key note: the action potential itself is sometimes called an electrochemical event, because it is produced by ions moving across the membrane, so c has a case; but the standard teaching line is electrical within a neuron and chemical between neurons, so a stands.", "topic": "Conduction within a neuron", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The electrical charge at resting potential across the neuron is", "options": {"a": "-70 millivolts", "b": "+70 millivolts", "c": "-40 millivolts", "d": "+40 millivolts"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the resting potential of a neuron is about -70 millivolts: the inside of the cell sits about 70 millivolts negative compared with the outside. A neuron that is not firing is said to be polarised. Textbooks round the figure to -70, though real neurons vary between roughly -60 and -80. Two features of the membrane produce it. First, ions are unevenly spread: sodium (Na+) is concentrated outside the cell, while potassium (K+) and large negative protein molecules are concentrated inside. Second, the resting membrane is leaky to potassium but almost closed to sodium, so potassium drifts out and leaves an excess of negative charge behind. The sodium-potassium pump, which uses energy, keeps the gradients steady by moving three sodium ions out for every two potassium ions in. If a stimulus nudges the membrane up to threshold (about -55 millivolts), sodium channels open and the voltage shoots up to a peak of about +30 to +40 millivolts, then falls back, briefly dips below rest (hyperpolarisation) and returns to -70. Example: Riya fixes it in memory as 'seventy, minus, inside': at rest the inside is 70 millivolts negative. Option a is right because -70 millivolts is the standard textbook value of the resting potential. Exam trap: +40 millivolts is the peak of the spike and -40 millivolts lies past threshold, so both are near-misses; and +70 has the sign the wrong way round.", "topic": "Resting membrane potential", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Neurotransmitters are present in the:", "options": {"a": "Soma", "b": "Synaptic terminals", "c": "Synaptic vesicles", "d": "Axon"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: neurotransmitters are held in synaptic vesicles, tiny membrane-bound sacs packed inside the axon terminals. A neurotransmitter is the chemical messenger that carries a signal across the synapse. Small-molecule transmitters such as acetylcholine and dopamine are made in the terminal and packed into vesicles there; larger neuropeptides are made in the cell body (soma) and carried down the axon. When an action potential reaches the terminal, calcium channels open, calcium flows in, and the vesicles fuse with the membrane and spill their contents into the synaptic cleft (exocytosis). The transmitter binds to receptors on the next neuron and is then cleared by reuptake or enzymes. In the early 1950s Bernard Katz and his colleagues showed that transmitter is released in fixed packets, or quanta, now understood as the contents of single vesicles; Katz shared the Nobel Prize in 1970. Example: when Kabir lifts his cricket bat, vesicles of acetylcholine in his motor neuron terminals release their load onto his arm muscles. Option c is right because the vesicles are the actual containers in which neurotransmitter is stored. Exam trap: a paper may ask where transmitters are released, and then 'axon terminals' is the answer; here the question asks where they are present, and the precise container is the vesicle. Key note: the vesicles sit inside the synaptic terminals, so b is not false; but the question asks where the transmitter is held, and the most precise answer is the vesicles, so c stands.", "topic": "Neurotransmitter storage", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following best defines Operational Definition", "options": {"a": "How a researcher will measure the variables", "b": "Testable explanation of the relationship between two variables", "c": "Identifying a problem to study", "d": "A factor that varies in amount and is measurable"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: an operational definition states how a researcher will measure (or produce) the variables in a study. Many psychological concepts, such as anxiety, intelligence or aggression, cannot be seen directly. An operational definition turns such a concept into something observable by naming the exact procedure, or operation, used to measure or manipulate it. Anxiety becomes the score on a named anxiety inventory; hunger becomes hours without food; aggression becomes the number of times a child hits a doll in ten minutes. The idea comes from the physicist Percy Bridgman (1927), whose operationism held that a concept means the set of operations used to measure it; Stanley Smith Stevens (1935) brought it into psychology. Operational definitions make research objective, replicable and testable; their limit is that one procedure captures only part of a construct. Example: Riya wants to study exam stress among Class 12 students in Jaipur. She defines exam stress as the score on a standard perceived stress scale taken one week before the board exams, plus resting heart rate measured that morning. Option a is right because an operational definition is exactly the recipe for measuring a variable. Exam trap: the distractors are definitions of neighbouring research terms placed side by side: a hypothesis (b), the first step of research (c) and a variable (d). A variable is the thing that varies; the operational definition is how you measure it.", "topic": "Operational definition", "fig": ""}, {"q": "When a relevant subgroups of the population are randomly sampled in proportion to its size, it is:", "options": {"a": "Random sample technique", "b": "Stratified random sample technique", "c": "Representative sample", "d": "Cluster sample technique"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: when relevant subgroups of a population are each randomly sampled in proportion to their size, the method is stratified random sampling. The researcher first divides the population into strata: subgroups that do not overlap, that together include everyone, and that are formed on a variable likely to matter for the study, such as sex, year of study, region or type of school. A separate random sample is then drawn from every stratum. In proportionate stratified sampling each stratum gets the same sampling fraction, so the sample mirrors the population on that variable. In disproportionate stratified sampling small strata are deliberately over-sampled so that each can be analysed on its own, and results are weighted back for the whole. Jerzy Neyman (1934) worked out optimum allocation, which takes more cases from strata that are larger or more variable. Because no subgroup can be left out or over-represented by bad luck, estimates are usually more precise than with a simple random sample of the same size. Example: Meera's college has 600 women and 400 men. For a sample of 100, she randomly picks 60 names from the women's list and 40 from the men's list. Option b is right because the stem describes both features: random selection within subgroups, and selection in proportion to their size. Exam trap: cluster sampling also starts by dividing the population into groups, but it randomly picks whole groups (schools, villages) and studies everyone in them, while stratified sampling takes some members from every group.", "topic": "Stratified random sampling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "When we say that the population mean is same as the sample mean, it signifies:", "options": {"a": "Research hypothesis", "b": "Alternative hypothesis", "c": "Experimental hypothesis", "d": "Null hypothesis"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: saying that the population mean is the same as the sample mean, so that there is no real difference, is the null hypothesis. A statistical test always pits two hypotheses against each other. The null hypothesis, written H0, is the 'nothing is going on' claim: no difference, no effect, no relationship. Here it says the sample comes from a population whose mean equals the value being compared, so any gap we see is just sampling error. The alternative hypothesis, H1, is the claim that a real difference exists. The term comes from Ronald Fisher (1935, The Design of Experiments): the null is the hypothesis the data may nullify. The logic runs backwards from what students expect. We assume H0 is true and ask how probable our result would be if it were. If that probability (the p value) is smaller than the alpha level, usually .05, we reject H0; if not, we fail to reject it, which is not the same as proving it. Example: Arjun asks whether the students at his coaching centre differ in average IQ from the population mean of 100. His H0: the centre's mean is 100. His H1: it is not 100. Option d is right because only the null hypothesis states equality, or no difference. Exam trap: research, alternative and experimental hypotheses all predict that a difference exists, the opposite of the stem; a statement of 'no difference' or 'equals' is the null. Key note: strictly, a null hypothesis is about population values (the population mean equals a stated value, or two population means are equal), not a claim that a sample mean equals the population mean, so the stem is loosely worded; it still describes 'no difference', so d stands.", "topic": "Null hypothesis", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Type II error is made by the researcher when:", "options": {"a": "The null hypothesis is true and the null hypothesis is rejected", "b": "The null hypothesis is true and the null hypothesis is accepted", "c": "The null hypothesis is false and the null hypothesis is accepted", "d": "The null hypothesis is false and the null hypothesis is rejected"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: a Type II error is made when the null hypothesis is false but the researcher accepts it (strictly, fails to reject it). Every significance test ends in a decision about the null hypothesis (H0, the claim of no effect), and the decision can be right or wrong. Retaining a true null and rejecting a false null are correct decisions. Rejecting a true null is a Type I error, a false positive: the researcher announces an effect that is not there. Its probability is alpha, fixed in advance, usually at .05. Retaining a false null is a Type II error, a false negative: a real effect goes undetected. Its probability is beta. The labels come from the framework of Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1928, 1933). Power, 1 minus beta, is the chance of catching a real effect; small samples and small effects lower it and so raise the risk of a Type II error. Example: Kabir tests whether a mindfulness course reduces exam anxiety. The course truly works, but with only 12 students the difference is not significant, and he concludes it has no effect: a Type II error. Option c is right because 'null false and accepted' is exactly a missed real effect. Exam trap: the two errors are easy to swap. Think of a smoke alarm: a Type I error is the alarm ringing with no fire; a Type II error is the alarm staying silent during a real fire.", "topic": "Type II error", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is a type of construct validity", "options": {"a": "Convergent validity", "b": "Criterion validity", "c": "Content validity", "d": "Concurrent validity"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: convergent validity is one of the main forms of evidence for construct validity. A construct is a trait that cannot be seen directly but is inferred from behaviour, such as anxiety, intelligence or extraversion. Construct validity asks whether a test truly measures that construct, and Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) argued that it is shown by checking whether scores relate to other things the way the theory predicts. Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959) made this concrete in the multitrait-multimethod matrix, which separates two kinds of evidence. Convergent validity: the test should correlate highly with other measures of the same construct, even ones using a different method (a self-report anxiety scale should agree with a clinician's anxiety rating). Discriminant, or divergent, validity: it should correlate weakly with measures of unrelated constructs (the anxiety scale should not track vocabulary). Factor analysis and expected group differences add further construct evidence. In the classic three-way scheme, the other two types are content validity and criterion-related validity, the latter split into concurrent and predictive. Example: Kabir builds a Hindi anxiety scale for adults. It correlates 0.70 with an established anxiety inventory (convergent evidence) and 0.05 with an arithmetic test (discriminant evidence). Option a is right because agreement with other measures of the same construct is, by definition, construct evidence. Exam trap: concurrent validity sounds almost like convergent validity, but it is a subtype of criterion validity, in which test scores are compared with a criterion measured at the same time, such as a current diagnosis.", "topic": "Convergent and construct validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In which measurement scale can we use all arithmetic operation i.e. addition, subtraction, multiplication and division", "options": {"a": "Nominal scale", "b": "Interval scale", "c": "Ratio scale", "d": "Ordinal scale"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: addition, subtraction, multiplication and division are all meaningful only on a ratio scale. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels, each adding one property. A nominal scale only names categories (blood group, a cricketer's shirt number); you can count cases and find the mode, but no arithmetic makes sense. An ordinal scale adds order (class rank, a five-point agreement rating); first is above second, but not by a known amount, so the median and percentiles suit it. An interval scale adds equal units, so differences mean the same everywhere (temperature in Celsius, and by convention IQ scores); you can add and subtract and compute a mean and standard deviation. But its zero is arbitrary, so ratios mislead: 20 degrees Celsius is not twice as hot as 10. A ratio scale adds a true zero that means none of the quantity at all (height, weight, reaction time, income, temperature in kelvin). Because zero is real, multiplication and division work too: a 20 kilogram bag really is twice as heavy as a 10 kilogram one. Example: Meera times students solving a puzzle. Arjun takes 30 seconds and Riya 60, so Riya took twice as long, a valid statement because time has a true zero. Option c is right because only the ratio scale supports meaningful multiplication and division as well as addition and subtraction. Exam trap: the interval scale also has equal units and allows addition and subtraction, so it tempts; without a true zero it cannot support ratios.", "topic": "Ratio scale of measurement", "fig": ""}, {"q": "All else being equal, which of the following signifies strongest correlation", "options": {"a": "-0.96", "b": "-0.24", "c": "+0.85", "d": "1.05"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: -0.96 is the strongest correlation among the possible values offered. A correlation coefficient, such as the product-moment r of Karl Pearson (1896), carries two separate pieces of information. Its sign shows the direction: positive means the two variables rise together, negative means one rises as the other falls. Its size, read without the sign (the absolute value), shows the strength: 0 means no straight-line relationship and 1 means a perfect one, with every point on a straight line. So r always lies between -1 and +1. Comparing by size, -0.96 is 0.96 away from zero, +0.85 is 0.85 and -0.24 only 0.24. The value 1.05 is impossible, because no correlation can go beyond +1 or -1. Squaring r gives the shared variance: 0.96 squared is about 0.92, so the two variables share about 92 percent of their variance, against about 72 percent for 0.85 and under 6 percent for 0.24. Example: Kabir finds that the hours his classmates spend gaming on weeknights correlate -0.96 with their attendance: the more they game, the less they attend, and the points lie almost on a downward line. Option a is right because strength is judged by distance from zero, not by sign, and -0.96 is the farthest from zero of the valid values. Exam trap: students treat a minus sign as weaker and pick +0.85, or pick 1.05 because it is the biggest number; the sign shows direction only, and no r can exceed 1.", "topic": "Strength of a correlation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The design which concerns the question of how many times items are to be observed and how the information and data gathered is to be analysed is called:", "options": {"a": "Sampling design", "b": "Statistical design", "c": "Observational design", "d": "Operational design"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the part of a research design that deals with how many items are to be observed and how the data gathered are to be analysed is the statistical design. C. R. Kothari (1985), in Research Methodology: Methods and Techniques, a standard Indian textbook, splits a research design into four parts. The sampling design is the method of selecting the items to be observed. The observational design covers the conditions under which observations are made. The statistical design fixes how many items are to be observed (sample size) and how the data will be analysed. The operational design covers the techniques by which the procedures of the other three are actually carried out. The stem is Kothari's definition of statistical design almost word for word ('how many times items' is a slip for 'how many items'). Example: Riya plans a survey of screen time among Pune college students. Picking students by stratified random selection is her sampling design; collecting questionnaires in classrooms in the first week of term is her observational design; fixing 300 students and planning a t-test between men and women is her statistical design; training assistants to hand out the forms is her operational design. Option b is right because sample size and analysis are exactly what the statistical design settles. Exam trap: sampling design sounds as if it should cover 'how many', but in this four-part split it is the method of selecting items, while the number of items and the analysis belong to the statistical design.", "topic": "Kothari's four research designs", "fig": "C. R. Kothari"}, {"q": "A data set has a positive skewness when,", "options": {"a": "Mean < Median < Mode", "b": "Mode < Median < Mean", "c": "Mode = Median = Mean", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a data set has positive skewness when mode < median < mean. Skewness is a lack of symmetry in a distribution, and it is named after its long tail. In a positive (right) skew most scores bunch at the low end and a few very high scores trail off to the right; incomes and the marks on a very hard test are classic cases. The three averages react differently to that tail. The mode, the most frequent score, stays at the peak of the hump. The median, the middle score, shifts a little towards the tail. The mean uses the value of every score, so the few extreme high scores drag it furthest to the right. Negative (left) skew is the mirror image, with a tail of low scores and mean < median < mode; in a symmetrical normal curve all three are equal. Karl Pearson (1895) measured skewness as the mean minus the mode, divided by the standard deviation, which comes out positive in a right skew. Example: in Arjun's village most families earn modest incomes, but two landowners earn crores, so the mean income is far above the median, and the median is above the most common income. Option b is right because in positive skew the mean is pulled up the most and the mode the least. Exam trap: option a is the order for negative skew, and students who only remember that the mean follows the tail can easily reverse it.", "topic": "Positive skewness", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Fritz Heider gave which of the given theories of Attitude", "options": {"a": "Cognitive Dissonance theory", "b": "Self-Perception theory", "c": "Balance theory", "d": "Persuasion theory"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: Fritz Heider (1946) gave the balance theory of attitudes. In 'Attitudes and cognitive organization' (1946), and more fully in The Psychology of Interpersonal Relations (1958), he argued that people want their attitudes and relationships to fit together harmoniously, which makes his a consistency theory. His model is the P-O-X triad: a person (P), another person (O) and an object, issue or third person (X), joined by positive relations (liking, agreeing) or negative ones (disliking). A triad is balanced when the product of the three signs is positive: all three positive, or two negative and one positive. It is imbalanced when the product is negative, and imbalance is felt as tension that pushes the person to restore balance by changing one of the relations, often an attitude. Example: Riya likes Kabir (+), Kabir loves cricket (+), but Riya dislikes cricket (-). The product is negative, so the triad is imbalanced. Riya may come to enjoy cricket, cool towards Kabir, or tell herself that he does not really care for the game. Heider also founded attribution theory, so his name turns up in two chapters of social psychology. Option c is right because balance theory, the P-O-X model, is Heider's contribution to attitude theory. Exam trap: cognitive dissonance theory is the best-known consistency theory and shares the idea of a pressure towards consistency, so it tempts; but it belongs to Leon Festinger (1957), not Heider.", "topic": "Heider's balance theory", "fig": "Fritz Heider"}, {"q": "'Repeated exposure to a stimulus translates into enhanced liking for the stimulus' is stated by:", "options": {"a": "Mere exposure hypothesis", "b": "Attractiveness stereotype", "c": "Spatial proximity", "d": "Need complementarity"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: the statement that repeated exposure to a stimulus raises liking for it is the mere exposure hypothesis, now usually called the mere exposure effect. Robert Zajonc (1968), in 'Attitudinal effects of mere exposure', presented nonsense words, Chinese-like characters and yearbook photographs of faces anywhere from 0 to 25 times, then asked people to guess how good the meaning of each word or character was, or how much they would like each man pictured. Liking rose steadily with the number of exposures, even though the items never changed and nothing pleasant was paired with them. In a striking follow-up, William Kunst-Wilson and Robert Zajonc (1980) flashed shapes too briefly to be recognised; people could not say which shapes they had seen, yet they preferred those shapes. So exposure can shape liking without conscious memory. One explanation is that familiar things are processed more easily, and that ease feels good and signals safety. The effect is largest for neutral stimuli and fades with very heavy repetition, when boredom sets in. Example: Kavya passes a new mural on her way to college every day. At first she finds it odd; a month later she likes it and is upset when it is painted over. Option a is right because the stem states exactly what Zajonc proposed: repetition alone increases liking. Exam trap: spatial proximity also increases liking, partly because people who live or sit near us are seen more often, so it is the near-miss; but proximity is about physical distance, and the stem names repeated exposure itself.", "topic": "Mere exposure effect", "fig": "Robert Zajonc"}, {"q": "When the likelihood to help in an emergency decreases because there are greater number of witnesses in an emergency, it is called", "options": {"a": "Social influence", "b": "Diffusion of responsibility", "c": "Negative state relief", "d": "Altruism"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: when helping becomes less likely because more people are watching, the cause is called diffusion of responsibility. Each witness feels only a fraction of the duty to act and assumes that someone else will. John Darley and Bibb Latane (1968) set out to test this after the 1964 killing of Kitty Genovese in New York, which newspapers blamed on the apathy of 38 onlooking neighbours (a number later shown to be inflated). In their intercom experiment, a student in a cubicle heard a fellow participant seem to have a seizure. Of students who thought they were the only listener, about 85 percent went for help before the seizure ended; of those who thought four others were also listening, only about 31 percent did. This drop in helping with group size is the bystander effect, and diffusion of responsibility is its main mechanism. In the decision model of Bibb Latane and John Darley (1970), a helper must notice the event, interpret it as an emergency, take personal responsibility, know what to do and decide to act; diffusion knocks out the responsibility step. Example: Rohan sees a cyclist fall on a busy Bengaluru road, thinks 'With this crowd, someone must have called an ambulance,' and walks on. Option b is right because it names exactly the shared, thinned-out responsibility the stem describes. Exam trap: social influence (pluralistic ignorance: everyone looks calm, so each decides nothing is wrong) is the other bystander process; it affects how the event is interpreted, not who feels responsible.", "topic": "Diffusion of responsibility", "fig": "John Darley and Bibb Latané"}, {"q": "Who among the following developed an equation to compare mental age to chronological age which is popularly called Intelligence Quotient?", "options": {"a": "Alfred Binet", "b": "Lewis Terman", "c": "William Stern", "d": "Theodore Simon"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: William Stern (1912) proposed the equation that compares mental age with chronological age, the quotient later called the intelligence quotient. The story starts with Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905), who built the first practical intelligence scale in Paris to identify children who needed special schooling. In their 1908 revision items were arranged by the age at which typical children pass them, so a child's mental age (MA) was the highest age level he or she could manage. But a gap between mental and real age means different things at different ages: a 4-year-old working at the level of a 2-year-old is far more delayed than a 14-year-old working at the level of a 12-year-old. Stern, a psychologist in Germany, fixed this by dividing MA by chronological age (CA), giving a ratio that means the same at every age, and named it the Intelligenzquotient. Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied the ratio by 100, giving whole numbers, and printed it as IQ in the Stanford-Binet, the American revision that spread the term worldwide. Example: Arjun is 8 and passes the items typical of 10-year-olds, so his ratio IQ is 10 divided by 8, times 100, which is 125. Option c is right because Stern was the first to express intelligence as the ratio of mental to chronological age. Exam trap: Binet supplies the mental age and Terman the multiplication by 100 and the popular name, so both are tempting near-misses. Key note: many books credit Lewis Terman, because his 1916 Stanford-Binet put the IQ (MA divided by CA, times 100) into wide use, so b has some support; but the equation comparing mental age with chronological age is William Stern's, so c stands.", "topic": "Origin of the IQ formula", "fig": "William Stern"}, {"q": "The tendency of people to behave differently on being knowingly observed is called:", "options": {"a": "Placebo effect", "b": "Halo effect", "c": "Horn effect", "d": "Hawthorne effect"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: changing one's behaviour because one knows one is being watched is called the Hawthorne effect. The name comes from a long series of studies (1924 to 1932) at the Hawthorne Works, a Western Electric telephone equipment factory near Chicago. Engineers first varied the lighting to see whether brighter light raised output. Output went up, but it also went up in a comparison group whose lighting never changed, and in some trials it stayed up even when the lights were dimmed. Later, in the relay assembly test room, a small team of women kept producing more through almost every change in rest pauses and hours. The workers seemed to respond to the special attention of being studied rather than to the changes themselves. The work is linked with Elton Mayo (1933) and was written up by Fritz Roethlisberger and William Dickson (1939) in Management and the Worker; the label was coined by Henry Landsberger (1958). Modern reanalyses find the data weaker than the legend, but the effect remains a standard threat to the validity of research. Example: when a district inspector sits in on lessons at Meera's school, every teacher arrives early and puts up charts, then slips back into old habits once the visit is over. Option d is right because the stem describes a change caused by awareness of being observed. Exam trap: the placebo effect also rests on expectation, but its source is the belief that one is being treated, not the knowledge that one is being watched.", "topic": "Hawthorne effect", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Wechsler tests have:", "options": {"a": "Verbal scale", "b": "Performance scale", "c": "Both a. and b.", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the classic Wechsler intelligence tests have both a verbal scale and a performance scale. David Wechsler (1939), chief psychologist at Bellevue Hospital in New York, found the Stanford-Binet poorly suited to adults: it leaned heavily on words and relied on mental age. His Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scale (1939) placed a Performance scale of non-verbal tasks beside the Verbal scale, and the Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (WISC, 1949) and Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS, 1955) kept the same plan. The Verbal subtests were information, comprehension, arithmetic, similarities, digit span and vocabulary; the Performance subtests were picture completion, picture arrangement, block design, object assembly and digit symbol. Each scale gave its own IQ, and together they gave a Full Scale IQ, all as deviation IQs (mean 100, SD 15). A large gap between Verbal and Performance IQ was read as a clinical clue. In India, Prabha Ramalingaswamy (1974) adapted the performance subtests for adults as the Wechsler Adult Performance Intelligence Scale (WAPIS). Example: Kabir, 30, who left school early, gets a Verbal IQ of 88 but a Performance IQ of 108, and his psychologist notes that limited schooling, not reasoning, is pulling the verbal score down. Option c is right because a verbal and a performance scale side by side is the defining design of the Wechsler tests. Exam trap: students who know that Wechsler added non-verbal tasks pick 'performance scale' alone. Key note: since the WISC-IV (2003) and WAIS-IV (2008), Verbal and Performance IQs have been replaced by four index scores (verbal comprehension, perceptual reasoning, working memory, processing speed); but the classic Wechsler scales had both, which is what this question means, so c stands.", "topic": "Wechsler verbal and performance scales", "fig": "David Wechsler"}, {"q": "_______ indicates how well a person performed on an IQ test relative to the particular age group the subject is a member of.", "options": {"a": "Ratio IQ", "b": "Deviation IQ", "c": "Both a. and b.", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: a deviation IQ tells you how well a person did compared with people of his or her own age group. David Wechsler (1939) brought it in with the Wechsler-Bellevue scale. The test is first normed on a large sample at each age; a person's raw score is then expressed as a distance from his or her age group's mean, in standard deviation (SD) units, and converted to a scale with a mean of 100 and an SD of 15; the Stanford-Binet used an SD of 16 until its fifth edition (2003). So 115 is one SD above the age mean, better than about 84 percent of age-mates, and 130 is two SD above, about the 98th percentile. Because each score is tied to the person's own age group, it means the same at 8 or at 48. The ratio IQ of William Stern (1912) and Lewis Terman (1916), mental age over chronological age times 100, works differently: it compares a person's mental age with his or her own age, and it breaks down in adulthood, when mental age stops growing but birthdays continue. Example: Arjun, 12, scores 85 on the WISC, one SD below the mean for his age, so he did better than only about 16 percent of 12-year-olds in the norm group. Option b is right because only the deviation IQ is defined by comparison with one's own age group. Exam trap: the ratio IQ also involves age, so 'both' looks safe; but it compares two ages within one person, not a person with peers.", "topic": "Deviation IQ", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a form of projective test", "options": {"a": "Rorschach inkblot test", "b": "Thematic Apperception test", "c": "Rotter Incomplete Sentences Blank", "d": "Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) is not a projective test; it is an objective self-report inventory. A projective test gives an ambiguous stimulus and freedom to answer in almost any way, so the answer is assumed to reveal the person's needs, conflicts and unconscious wishes. Lawrence Frank (1939) coined the term projective methods for such tools. The Rorschach test of Hermann Rorschach (1921) uses ten inkblots; the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) of Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray (1935) asks for stories about vague pictures; and the Incomplete Sentences Blank of Julian Rotter and Janet Rafferty (1950) asks the person to finish 40 sentence stems. An objective inventory uses clear statements, fixed answer choices and keyed scoring. The MMPI, built by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943), has about 550 true or false statements scored on 10 clinical scales plus validity scales (L, F and K) that catch faking and careless answering. Its items were empirically keyed, chosen because patient groups answered them differently. Example: Kabir marks 'I wake up tired most mornings' as true and is compared with norms; Meera tells a story about a TAT picture and the psychologist reads her needs into it. Option d is right because the MMPI's fixed true or false format and norm-based scoring make it the classic objective test, the opposite of a projective technique. Exam trap: sentence completion looks structured, so students wrongly pick it; it is a completion technique, one of Gardner Lindzey's (1959) five families of projective methods; its stems add some structure, but it remains projective.", "topic": "Projective versus objective tests", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"Barnum effect\" refers to the:", "options": {"a": "The tendency of people to accept and approve of the interpretation of their personalities that you give them", "b": "The tendency of people to not accept and disapprove of the interpretation of their personalities that you give them", "c": "The tendency of people to remain neutral of the interpretation of their personalities that you give them", "d": "The tendency of people to seek more information before they accept or disapprove of the interpretation of their personalities that you give them"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Barnum effect is the tendency of people to accept, and approve of, a general personality interpretation as an accurate description of themselves. Such statements feel personal but fit almost everyone ('You have a great need for others to like you'). Bertram Forer (1949) demonstrated it in his class. He gave students a personality test, then handed each one the same 'individual' sketch, put together largely from an astrology book. Asked how well it described them, they rated it on average 4.26 out of 5. Paul Meehl (1956) gave the effect its name, after the showman P. T. Barnum, whose shows were said to have a little something for everybody; it is also called the Forer effect. Acceptance rises when the sketch is flattering, seems prepared for the person alone and comes from an authority; hence the appeal of horoscopes and palm readers. In assessment, a client nodding along to a report is no proof that the report is valid. Example: Riya reads her weekly horoscope, 'you are independent but sometimes doubt your own decisions', and feels it was written for her, though it fits each of her friends just as well. Option a is right because the Barnum effect is, by definition, uncritical acceptance of a general interpretation as one's own. Exam trap: examiners reverse the definition (rejecting the interpretation) or offer a cautious middle path (staying neutral, seeking more information); the effect is about believing too readily, not doubting.", "topic": "Barnum (Forer) effect", "fig": "Bertram Forer"}, {"q": "Which neurological disorder is characterized by the degeneration of dopamine producing neurons in the substantia nigra?", "options": {"a": "Alzheimer's disease", "b": "Parkinson's disease", "c": "Multiple sclerosis", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: Parkinson's disease is the neurological disorder in which the dopamine-producing neurons of the substantia nigra degenerate. The substantia nigra ('black substance', dark with the pigment neuromelanin) is a nucleus in the midbrain. Its pars compacta sends dopamine along the nigrostriatal pathway to the striatum (caudate nucleus and putamen), part of the basal ganglia, which help to start movements and keep them smooth. James Parkinson (1817) described the illness as the 'shaking palsy'. Signs usually appear only after roughly 60 to 80 percent of the nigral dopamine cells have been lost: a resting tremor (often 'pill-rolling'), rigidity, bradykinesia (slowness of movement), a shuffling gait, a mask-like face and poor balance. Oleh Hornykiewicz (1960) found that patients' striatum was almost empty of dopamine, and the surviving nigral cells contain Lewy bodies, clumps of the protein alpha-synuclein. Treatment uses L-dopa, a precursor that, unlike dopamine itself, crosses the blood-brain barrier. Example: Kabir notices that his grandmother's thumb and fingers rub together, as if rolling a pill, while she watches television, that her face has become expressionless, and that she needs several tries to rise from a chair; these point to Parkinson's disease. Option b is right because the loss of nigral dopamine neurons is the defining pathology of Parkinson's disease. Exam trap: students reverse the dopamine story. Too little dopamine in the nigrostriatal pathway gives Parkinson's disease, while too much dopamine activity in the mesolimbic pathway is linked with schizophrenia. Alzheimer's disease, the other famous neurodegenerative illness, is tied to acetylcholine loss, not nigral dopamine.", "topic": "Parkinson's disease and dopamine", "fig": "James Parkinson"}, {"q": "Rajat is experiencing sudden episodes of intense dread along with shortness of breath, heart palpitations which come and go. He is suffering from:", "options": {"a": "Generalised anxiety disorder", "b": "Obsessive Compulsive Disorder", "c": "Panic Disorder", "d": "Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: Rajat's sudden episodes of intense dread with shortness of breath and palpitations, which come and go, describe panic disorder. In DSM-5 (2013) a panic attack is an abrupt surge of intense fear or discomfort that peaks within minutes and brings at least 4 of 13 symptoms: pounding heart, sweating, trembling, shortness of breath, a choking feeling, chest pain, nausea, dizziness, chills or heat, numbness or tingling, feelings of unreality, fear of losing control or 'going crazy', and fear of dying. Panic disorder is diagnosed when unexpected attacks recur, coming 'out of the blue', and at least one attack is followed by a month or more of persistent worry about further attacks or a maladaptive change in behaviour, such as avoiding exercise. David Clark (1986) explained panic as catastrophic misinterpretation of bodily sensations: a racing heart is read as a heart attack, fear rises, the heart races faster, and the spiral peaks in panic. CBT with interoceptive exposure (deliberately bringing on the feared sensations) and SSRIs are first-line treatments. Rajat's episodes are sudden, intense, brief and recurrent, with the classic bodily signs; nothing points to obsessions, trauma or constant worry. Option c is right because recurring, discrete surges of fear with bodily symptoms are the core of panic disorder. Exam trap: generalised anxiety disorder is the near miss, since it also involves anxiety and bodily tension; but GAD is steady, free-floating worry on most days for at least six months, not attacks that peak within minutes and pass.", "topic": "Panic disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of these is not a part of Cluster B personality disorders", "options": {"a": "Narcissistic personality disorder", "b": "Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder", "c": "Histrionic personality disorder", "d": "Borderline personality disorder"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: obsessive-compulsive personality disorder (OCPD) is not a Cluster B disorder; it belongs to Cluster C. A personality disorder is an enduring, inflexible pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates from cultural expectations, begins by early adulthood and causes distress or impairment. DSM-5 (2013) groups its ten specific personality disorders into three clusters by surface resemblance. Cluster A is odd or eccentric: paranoid, schizoid and schizotypal. Cluster B is dramatic, emotional or erratic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic and narcissistic. Cluster C is anxious or fearful: avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive. A simple memory aid is 'weird, wild, worried'. OCPD is a pervasive preoccupation with orderliness, perfectionism, rules and control, at the cost of flexibility, openness and efficiency. This tight, anxious over-control fits the fearful Cluster C, not the dramatic, attention-seeking style of Cluster B. Example: Meera spends weekends drawing up colour-coded schedules, insists the family load the dishwasher her way, and cannot finish her thesis because no chapter is ever perfect; that is the OCPD pattern, and it feels right to her. Option b is right because narcissistic, histrionic and borderline disorders all sit in Cluster B, and OCPD is the only one that does not, so it is the odd one out. Exam trap: do not confuse OCPD with obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). OCD has true obsessions and compulsions that the person usually experiences as unwanted (ego-dystonic), and DSM-5 places it in its own chapter of obsessive-compulsive and related disorders. OCPD has no true obsessions or rituals, and its traits feel right to the person (ego-syntonic).", "topic": "Personality disorder clusters", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Shashi witnessed brutal killings, and screams of children during war. After returning, he suffered from recurring haunting memories and nightmares, a numbed social withdrawal, jumpy anxiety, and insomnia. He may be experiencing:", "options": {"a": "Post-Traumatic Stress disorder", "b": "Generalised Anxiety Disorder", "c": "Dissociative Disorder", "d": "None of the Above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: Shashi's symptoms after witnessing killings in war fit post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). Soldiers' breakdowns were once called shell shock, a term used by Charles Myers (1915), and later combat fatigue; PTSD entered the manuals only with DSM-III (1980), largely through work with Vietnam veterans. DSM-5 (2013) first requires exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence, by experiencing it, witnessing it or learning that it happened to someone close. Four symptom clusters follow. Intrusion: unwanted memories, nightmares, flashbacks, distress at reminders. Avoidance: of trauma-related thoughts, places and people. Negative changes in thinking and mood: guilt, blame, detachment from others, loss of interest and inability to feel positive emotions (emotional numbing). Changes in arousal and reactivity: irritability, hypervigilance, an exaggerated startle response, poor concentration and disturbed sleep. Symptoms must last more than one month; from 3 days to 1 month the diagnosis is acute stress disorder. Trauma-focused CBT, prolonged exposure (developed by Edna Foa) and eye movement desensitisation and reprocessing (EMDR; Francine Shapiro, 1989) are first-line treatments. Shashi meets every part: the trauma (witnessed killings), intrusion (haunting memories, nightmares), negative mood (numbed withdrawal) and arousal (jumpiness, insomnia). Option a is right because a qualifying trauma followed by intrusions, numbing and hyperarousal is the textbook picture of PTSD. Exam trap: generalised anxiety disorder also brings anxiety and poor sleep, and 'dissociative' sounds right for flashbacks; but GAD needs no trauma and has no re-experiencing, and flashbacks inside PTSD do not make it a separate dissociative disorder.", "topic": "Post-traumatic stress disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is a subtype of schizophrenia?", "options": {"a": "Catatonia", "b": "Paranoid", "c": "Undifferentiated", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: catatonic, paranoid and undifferentiated are all classic subtypes of schizophrenia, so 'all of the above' is correct. Emil Kraepelin (1899) brought together three conditions described earlier, hebephrenia, catatonia and paranoid forms, as varieties of one illness, dementia praecox. Eugen Bleuler (1911) renamed it schizophrenia. DSM-IV (1994) and DSM-IV-TR (2000) listed five subtypes by the most prominent symptoms. Paranoid type: prominent delusions and voices, with thinking fairly intact. Disorganised type: disorganised speech and behaviour with flat or inappropriate affect. Catatonic type: motor signs such as stupor, posturing or excitement. Undifferentiated type: criteria met but no single picture dominates. Residual type: active symptoms gone, mild or negative symptoms remain. DSM-5 (2013) dropped all the subtypes because they were unreliable, unstable over time and did not predict treatment response, and made catatonia a specifier that can be added to schizophrenia and other disorders. Example: Kabir stands mute in one odd posture for hours and holds his arm wherever the nurse places it (waxy flexibility); under DSM-IV he would have been 'catatonic type', and under DSM-5 he has 'schizophrenia, with catatonia'. Option d is right because each of options a, b and c names a recognised classic subtype. Exam trap: students who know that DSM-5 abolished the subtypes may refuse every option; others pick the single most famous one (paranoid) and miss 'all of the above'. Key note: DSM-5 and ICD-11 no longer use subtypes, and 'catatonia' is now a specifier rather than the 'catatonic type'; but all three are the classic subtypes this question means, so d stands.", "topic": "Subtypes of schizophrenia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a positive symptom of Schizophrenia?", "options": {"a": "Hallucinations", "b": "Talk in disorganized and deluded ways", "c": "Exhibit inappropriate laughter", "d": "Toneless voices"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: a toneless voice is not a positive symptom of schizophrenia; it is a negative symptom. Positive symptoms are excesses or distortions, something added to normal experience: hallucinations (usually voices), delusions, disorganised speech and grossly disorganised behaviour, including inappropriate affect such as laughing while describing a death. Negative symptoms are losses or reductions of normal function: affective flattening or diminished emotional expression (an unmoving face, a monotonous voice), alogia (poverty of speech), avolition (loss of goal-directed activity), anhedonia and asociality. Timothy Crow (1980) linked positive symptoms (Type I) with dopamine overactivity and good drug response, and negative symptoms (Type II) with structural brain changes. Nancy Andreasen rated them on separate scales, the SANS (1982) and the SAPS (1984). Example: Meera believes the television sends her secret messages, a positive symptom. Her mother notices that Meera now answers in a flat, unchanging tone and her face stays blank even at good news; that toneless speech is negative. Option d is right because a toneless voice reflects reduced emotional expression, a loss of normal function, which places it among the negative symptoms. Exam trap: inappropriate laughter and flat affect sound alike but fall on opposite sides. Inappropriate affect is emotion present but wrong for the situation; flat affect is emotion missing. Key note: some texts, following Peter Liddle (1987), list inappropriate affect under a third, disorganised group rather than with the positive symptoms; either way it is not a deficit, and a toneless voice is the classic negative symptom, so d stands.", "topic": "Positive and negative symptoms", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of these illnesses is related to abnormality in the dopaminergic system?", "options": {"a": "Schizophrenia", "b": "Parkinson", "c": "Alzheimer", "d": "Schizophrenia and Parkinson"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: both schizophrenia and Parkinson's disease are linked to abnormality in the dopaminergic system. Dopamine is a catecholamine neurotransmitter carried along several pathways. The nigrostriatal pathway runs from the substantia nigra to the striatum and controls movement; the mesolimbic pathway runs from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens and limbic areas and handles reward and salience. In Parkinson's disease the nigrostriatal neurons die, so there is too little dopamine, giving tremor, rigidity and slowness; L-dopa replaces it. In schizophrenia the dopamine hypothesis holds that too much dopamine activity in the mesolimbic pathway produces positive symptoms. Arvid Carlsson and Margit Lindqvist (1963) proposed that antipsychotic drugs work by blocking monoamine receptors, Philip Seeman and colleagues (1976) showed that their clinical potency tracks how strongly they bind dopamine receptors of the kind later named D2, and amphetamine, which releases dopamine, can cause a paranoid psychosis. The two illnesses are mirror images. Example: Meera's uncle, treated for schizophrenia with haloperidol, a strong dopamine blocker, develops stiff arms, a tremor and a shuffling walk (extrapyramidal side effects); in the other direction, too much L-dopa can bring on hallucinations in Parkinson's patients. Option d is right because Parkinson's disease involves too little dopamine and schizophrenia too much, so both are dopaminergic disorders and the option naming both is the complete answer. Exam trap: options a and b are each true but incomplete; Alzheimer's disease is the neurodegenerative lure, but its core transmitter loss is acetylcholine.", "topic": "Dopamine in schizophrenia and Parkinson's", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A person who is impatient, compulsive and competitive is likely to be diagnosed as having", "options": {"a": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder", "b": "Type A personality", "c": "Type B personality", "d": "Machiavellian personality"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: a person who is impatient, compulsive and competitive fits the Type A personality, or more exactly the Type A behaviour pattern. The idea came from two San Francisco cardiologists, Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman (1959), who noticed that many of their heart patients were driven, hurried people. They described Type A as intense competitive striving for achievement, a chronic sense of time urgency (impatience, doing several things at once, irritation at any delay), easily roused hostility and over-commitment to work. Type B people are relaxed, patient and easy-going. In their Western Collaborative Group Study of about 3,000 working men, followed through the 1960s, Type A men developed coronary heart disease about twice as often as Type B men. Later studies found that hostility, especially cynical hostility, is the harmful core, while time urgency on its own matters less. Example: Rohan finishes other people's sentences, drums his fingers in every queue, checks email at red lights and treats an office cricket match as a battle; he shows the Type A pattern. Option b is right because impatience (time urgency), competitiveness and a driven, compulsive work style are the textbook marks of Type A. Exam trap: the word 'compulsive' pulls students towards obsessive-compulsive disorder; but OCD is about obsessions and rituals, and 'compulsive' here means driven and unable to slow down. Key note: Type A is a behaviour pattern, not a diagnosis in DSM or ICD, so 'diagnosed' in the stem is loose; it still names the pattern described, so b stands.", "topic": "Type A behaviour pattern", "fig": "Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman"}, {"q": "The most acceptable criterion of abnormality is the", "options": {"a": "Personality", "b": "Pathological", "c": "Sociability", "d": "Statistical"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: the statistical criterion, deviation from the average, is the classic and most widely cited single criterion of abnormality. It calls a behaviour or trait abnormal if it is rare, far from the population average. On the normal curve, scores in the extreme tails, say more than two standard deviations from the mean, count as deviant. Intellectual disability is partly defined this way: an IQ of about 70 or below lies two standard deviations under the mean of 100. It is objective and easy to measure. Its weakness is that rarity is not illness: a genius IQ is just as rare, and common problems such as depression and anxiety are not rare at all. Modern texts weigh it together with social norms, distress, dysfunction and danger (the 'four Ds': deviance, distress, dysfunction, danger). Example: Kabir sleeps only four hours a night, far less than almost everyone, so he is statistically deviant; yet if he feels fine and works well, few clinicians would call him disordered. Option d is right because, of the four options, only statistical deviation is a recognised criterion of abnormality. Exam trap: 'pathological' sounds medical and authoritative, but it is the very thing being defined, so it cannot serve as the defining criterion. Key note: no single criterion is accepted on its own, and modern texts put dysfunction and distress at the core; among these options, statistical deviation is the only real criterion and is the answer the official Gurugram key gives for the same question, so d stands.", "topic": "Criteria of abnormality", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"A patient was highly disturbed by a morbid fear of travelling in a train, but he did not know why.\" Which type of disease the patient is suffering from?", "options": {"a": "Psychosomatic disorder", "b": "Psychoneurosis", "c": "Psychosis", "d": "Fatigue"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: a morbid fear of travelling by train, whose cause the patient cannot explain, is a phobia, and in the older classification a phobia is a psychoneurosis. In the 1890s Sigmund Freud used the term psychoneurosis for non-psychotic disorders that he traced to unconscious conflict. Older systems, including DSM-I (1952) and DSM-II (1968), separated the psychoneuroses from the psychoses. Psychoneuroses bring marked distress, but the person stays in contact with reality, usually knows the fear is unreasonable and keeps his personality largely intact; examples were anxiety, phobic and obsessive-compulsive neuroses, hysteria and neurasthenia. Psychoses involve loss of contact with reality and often no insight. Freud explained phobias as anxiety displaced onto a symbolic object, as in his case of Little Hans (1909), whose fear of horses he traced to fear of the father; 'he did not know why' echoes that idea of an unconscious source. Example: Vikram takes long bus routes to avoid trains and admits the fear is silly but cannot explain it; he is distressed yet in touch with reality. Option b is right because an irrational, distressing fear with intact reality contact is the classic picture of a psychoneurosis (phobic neurosis). Exam trap: 'morbid' and 'highly disturbed' tempt students towards psychosis, but severity of distress does not make a disorder psychotic; only loss of reality contact does. Key note: DSM-III (1980) dropped the word neurosis, and today this would be a specific phobia (situational type) within the anxiety disorders; but the stem uses the old classification, in which a phobia is a psychoneurosis, so b stands.", "topic": "Psychoneurosis versus psychosis", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which technique is none other than a de-conditioning or counter conditioning procedure used to breakdown neurotic anxiety reaction in the model of experimental neurosis", "options": {"a": "Psychotherapy", "b": "Non-directive therapy", "c": "Systematic desensitization", "d": "Milieu therapy"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: systematic desensitisation is the deconditioning, or counterconditioning, procedure that grew out of breaking down neurotic anxiety in animals with experimental neurosis. In Ivan Pavlov's laboratory in the 1920s, dogs forced to tell a circle from an ever more circular ellipse broke down. In the late 1940s Joseph Wolpe gave cats electric shocks in a small cage until they were too anxious to eat there even when hungry. He then fed them in a room very unlike the cage, then in rooms more and more like it, and finally in the cage itself, until the anxiety was gone. Eating, incompatible with anxiety, had replaced the fear: counterconditioning. Wolpe called the principle reciprocal inhibition, borrowing the term from Charles Sherrington (1906): two incompatible responses cannot occur together. In Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958) he applied it to people, with deep muscle relaxation (from Edmund Jacobson, 1929) as the response that inhibits anxiety. The method has three steps: relaxation training, an anxiety hierarchy rated in subjective units of disturbance (SUDS), and graded exposure, usually imagined, moving up only when each step is calm. Example: Arjun, afraid of injections, relaxes while imagining a syringe in a picture, then a nurse preparing one, then the needle touching his arm. Option c is right because systematic desensitisation is precisely Wolpe's counterconditioning method, derived from his cure of experimental neurosis in cats. Exam trap: flooding and exposure with response prevention also treat anxiety, but they work through extinction and habituation without a competing response; counterconditioning by reciprocal inhibition is Wolpe's systematic desensitisation.", "topic": "Systematic desensitisation", "fig": "Joseph Wolpe"}, {"q": "Nowadays biofeedback is being used in the treatment of", "options": {"a": "Paranoia", "b": "Catatonia", "c": "Phobia", "d": "Psychosomatic disorder and Epilepsy"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: biofeedback is used today mainly for psychosomatic (psychophysiological) disorders and, as EEG neurofeedback, for epilepsy. In biofeedback a sensor measures a body process that a person normally cannot sense, such as muscle tension, skin temperature or brain waves, and displays it as a tone or graph. The person learns by trial and error to move the reading in a healthy direction, each success reinforced by the signal. The idea rests on Neal Miller (1969), who reported that autonomic responses, long thought to be involuntary, could be operantly conditioned in rats; the rat results later proved hard to replicate. Electromyographic (EMG) feedback of forehead muscle tension treats tension headache; thermal feedback, learning to warm the hands, treats migraine and Raynaud's disease. Barry Sterman found in the late 1960s that cats trained to produce a sensorimotor rhythm resisted drug-induced seizures, and EEG neurofeedback was then applied to people with epilepsy. Example: Meera has migraines. With a sensor on her finger, she watches a display of her skin temperature and learns, through calm imagery, to warm her hands, and her attacks grow rarer. Option d is right because psychosomatic disorders and epilepsy are the classic, best-supported uses of biofeedback. Exam trap: phobia tempts students because biofeedback teaches relaxation, but phobias are treated mainly by exposure and systematic desensitisation. Key note: biofeedback-assisted relaxation is also used as an aid in anxiety and phobias, so c has some support; but the classic textbook uses are psychosomatic disorders and epilepsy, which matches the official Gurugram answer, so d stands.", "topic": "Uses of biofeedback", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Psychotherapy in which the therapist prescribes remedies and courses of action as much as a physician prescribes medicine, used extensively in the early history of psychotherapy is known as", "options": {"a": "Psychotherapy", "b": "Directive therapy", "c": "Directive counseling", "d": "Non directive therapy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: directive therapy is psychotherapy in which the therapist takes charge, diagnoses the problem and prescribes remedies and courses of action, much as a physician prescribes medicine. The therapist is the expert: he gathers the facts, decides what is wrong and tells the client what to do, using advice, persuasion, suggestion and reassurance. This was the dominant style in the early history of psychotherapy: hypnosis and suggestion in the late nineteenth century, and the rational 'persuasion therapy' of the Swiss physician Paul Dubois (1904). The label became common after Carl Rogers (1942), in Counseling and Psychotherapy, called his own method non-directive, because his counsellor does not advise or steer. Directive counselling is a related but narrower idea: Edmund G. Williamson (1939, How to Counsel Students) set out a counsellor-centred method for students' educational and vocational problems in six steps: analysis, synthesis, diagnosis, prognosis, counselling and follow-up. Example: Meera says she cannot sleep before exams; her therapist diagnoses exam anxiety and prescribes a fixed bedtime, a revision timetable and breathing exercises. Option b is right because the stem describes a form of psychotherapy, therapist-led and prescriptive like medicine, that dominated its early history: directive therapy. Exam trap: directive counselling (option c) shares the word and the leading role, but it is Williamson's guidance method for normal students, not the early psychotherapy the stem describes. Key note: Anukriti's key table gives b, but her worked solution names c (directive counselling); the two ideas are close, but the stem asks for a psychotherapy, and directive counselling is Williamson's school and vocational guidance approach, so b stands.", "topic": "Directive therapy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which psychotherapy was developed by Perls that attempts to restore a person's sense of wholeness until the individual becomes strong enough for growth to take place?", "options": {"a": "Gestalt therapy", "b": "Yoga therapy", "c": "Family therapy", "d": "Psychoanalytical therapy"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: Gestalt therapy, developed by Fritz Perls (1951), aims to restore a person's sense of wholeness so that growth can take place. Perls, a German-born psychoanalyst, built the approach with his wife Laura Perls and the writer Paul Goodman; the founding book, Gestalt Therapy (1951), was by Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Goodman. The German word Gestalt means a whole or complete form. Perls held that people become fragmented: they disown parts of themselves, carry 'unfinished business' from the past, and live in their heads instead of in present experience. Therapy aims at awareness and integration: reowning the split-off parts until the person is whole and self-supporting enough to mature. It stays in the here and now. The therapist asks 'what' and 'how' rather than 'why', and asks clients to take responsibility ('I won't' instead of 'I can't'). Its best-known technique is the empty chair: the client speaks to an absent person, or to a part of the self, then switches chairs to answer. Example: Riya still resents her elder sister for leaving home without a goodbye. In the empty chair she tells her sister how abandoned she felt, then answers as the sister, and the anger she had disowned becomes hers again. Option a is right because Gestalt therapy is Perls's, and its aim is integration of the fragmented self so that growth can follow. Exam trap: Gestalt therapy is not Gestalt psychology, the school of perception founded by Max Wertheimer (1912) with Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka; Perls only borrowed its idea of the whole.", "topic": "Gestalt therapy", "fig": "Fritz Perls"}, {"q": "A state of apprehension, tension and worry along with fear is characterized as", "options": {"a": "Anxiety", "b": "Depression", "c": "Phobia", "d": "Schizophrenia"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: a state of apprehension, tension and worry, accompanied by fear, is anxiety. Anxiety is a future-oriented emotion: the sense that something bad may happen, felt as worry in the mind, tension and arousal in the body. DSM-5 (2013) separates it from fear. Fear is the alarm reaction to a definite danger that is present now, such as a snake on the path; anxiety is apprehension about a possible future threat, such as worrying about snakes on tomorrow's trek. Anxiety has three parts: cognitive (worry, 'what if' thoughts), physiological (racing heart, muscle tension, sweating, poor sleep) and behavioural (avoidance, checking, reassurance seeking). In moderate amounts it is normal and useful; the law of Robert Yerkes and John Dodson (1908) shows that moderate arousal helps performance. It becomes a disorder when it is out of proportion to the danger, persistent and impairing. Charles Spielberger's State-Trait Anxiety Inventory (1970) separates state anxiety, how anxious one feels right now, from trait anxiety, a lasting tendency to feel anxious. Example: Arjun's job interview is a month away. Each night he lies awake picturing every question going wrong, his shoulders ache and his stomach churns at breakfast, though nothing bad has happened yet; that is anxiety. Option a is right because apprehension, tension and worry together form the definition of anxiety, with fear as its emotional colour. Exam trap: phobia also contains fear, so it lures students; but a phobia is fear tied to a specific object or situation, whereas the stem describes a general state without any named object.", "topic": "Anxiety and fear", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Specific phobias are defined as:", "options": {"a": "Excessive worry bouts triggered by a specific object or situation", "b": "An abnormal sensitivity to light", "c": "An excessive, unreasonable, persistent fear triggered by a specific object or situation", "d": "A persistent fear of social situations"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: specific phobias are defined as an excessive, unreasonable and persistent fear triggered by a specific object or situation. In DSM-5 (2013) a specific phobia is marked fear or anxiety about a particular object or situation; the cue almost always provokes immediate fear; it is actively avoided or endured with intense distress; the fear is out of proportion to the actual danger; and it lasts typically six months or more, causing distress or impairment. DSM-5 lists five types: animal (dogs, spiders, snakes), natural environment (heights, storms, water), blood-injection-injury, situational (lifts, flying, enclosed spaces) and other (choking, vomiting). The blood-injection-injury type is unusual: heart rate and blood pressure rise and then fall sharply, so the person may faint. DSM-IV (1994) described the fear as 'excessive or unreasonable' and required the person to recognise this; DSM-5 dropped the insight rule and lets the clinician judge proportion, but 'unreasonable' and 'irrational' survive in exam wording. Example: Ananya has been terrified of dogs since one chased her at six. She crosses the road whenever she sees one, even a puppy on a leash, though she knows it cannot hurt her; that is an animal-type specific phobia. Option c is right because it states every defining feature: a fear that is excessive, unreasonable and persistent, cued by one specific object or situation. Exam trap: option d also describes a phobia, but fear of social situations and scrutiny is social anxiety disorder (social phobia), a separate DSM-5 diagnosis; and option a swaps fear for worry, the mark of generalised anxiety disorder.", "topic": "Specific phobia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is a predominant evolutionary theory of phobias?", "options": {"a": "Non-associative fear acquisition", "b": "Learned fear responses", "c": "Biological preparedness", "d": "Specific phobia acquisition"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: biological preparedness is the predominant evolutionary theory of phobias. Martin Seligman (1971) argued that evolution has prepared humans to learn some fears far more easily than others. Fears of snakes, spiders, heights, darkness and deep water protected our ancestors, so brains that learned them quickly had a survival advantage. Seligman built on John Garcia and Robert Koelling's (1966) finding that rats readily link a taste, but not a light or sound, with sickness. Prepared fears are learned fast, often in one trial, resist extinction, feel irrational and cluster around ancestral dangers. In the 1970s Arne Ohman and colleagues found that fear conditioned to snake and spider pictures outlasts fear conditioned to flowers or mushrooms. Michael Cook and Susan Mineka (1989) found that laboratory-reared monkeys learned fear of toy snakes, but not of flowers, by watching videos of frightened monkeys. Example: after one large spider drops onto Kabir's pillow, he cannot sleep in any room until it has been checked, yet he plugs in a frayed phone charger without a second thought. Option c is right because preparedness is the classic evolutionary account of why phobias cluster around ancestral dangers. Exam trap: option b is the tempting phrase, but plain conditioning has no evolutionary content: it says how a fear is acquired, not why the feared objects are so often spiders and heights rather than sockets and cars. Key note: the non-associative model (Richie Poulton and Ross Menzies, 2002) is also evolutionary, holding that some fears are innate and appear without any conditioning, so a has a case; but preparedness is the classic, predominant evolutionary account, so c stands.", "topic": "Biological preparedness and phobias", "fig": "Martin Seligman"}, {"q": "It is considered that successful CBT treatments of social phobia include elements of", "options": {"a": "Exposure therapy", "b": "Social skills training", "c": "Cognitive restructuring", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: successful CBT for social phobia combines exposure, social skills training and cognitive restructuring, so 'all of the above' is correct. Social anxiety disorder (social phobia) is intense fear of situations in which one may be watched and judged. In the cognitive model of David Clark and Adrian Wells (1995), the person enters a social situation expecting to be judged, turns attention inward to monitor a negative self-image ('I'm visibly shaking'), and uses safety behaviours (avoiding eye contact, rehearsing sentences) that prevent him from learning his fears are exaggerated. Treatment attacks each link. Exposure means graded, repeated entry into feared situations, set up as behavioural experiments with safety behaviours dropped. Cognitive restructuring identifies and tests distorted thoughts such as 'everyone will notice I'm nervous and think I'm stupid'. Social skills training, through modelling, role-play and feedback, helps where real skill deficits keep the problem going. Richard Heimberg's cognitive behavioural group therapy, tested against a credible placebo in 1990, delivers exposure and cognitive restructuring in groups. Example: Kabir dreads speaking in seminars. He challenges the thought 'I will go blank and everyone will laugh', rehearses with his therapist, then presents to a small group without his script, and on video sees he looked calmer than he felt. Option d is right because each listed element targets a different maintaining factor, and effective programmes use them together. Exam trap: students who know that exposure is the most powerful ingredient pick option a, but the question asks what successful programmes include, and they include all three.", "topic": "CBT for social anxiety", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"gurugram-2025-solved": [{"q": "A music student learned 'raag malkaus' and then, a month later, learned 'raag kaafi'. Every time he tried to sing 'raag kaafi' he began singing 'raag malkaus'. This is known as", "concept": "The answer is c: the student is showing proactive interference, because the raag he learnt first (malkaus) keeps intruding when he tries to recall the raag he learnt later (kaafi). Interference theory holds that we forget mainly because other memories compete with the one we want, not because traces simply fade with time; John McGeoch (1932) made this case against the old law of disuse. Interference works in two directions. In proactive interference, old learning works forwards and disrupts recall of newer learning. In retroactive interference, new learning works backwards and disrupts recall of older learning. A memory aid: PRO means forwards (old spoils new), RETRO means backwards (new spoils old). Benton Underwood (1957) showed how powerful the proactive kind is: people who had already learnt many lists in the laboratory forgot a fresh list far faster over 24 hours than people who had learnt none. Interference is strongest when the two sets of material are similar, which is why two raags with overlapping phrases clash. Another example: Riya changes her phone number and for weeks recites the old one when asked for the new one; that is proactive. Option c is right because the earlier raag is blocking the later one, the classic old-disrupts-new pattern. Exam trap: examiners reverse the two definitions, so always ask which memory is being lost. Here the newer raag kaafi is lost and the older raag is the culprit, so it is proactive.", "traps": {"a": "The method of loci is a mnemonic credited to the Greek poet Simonides, in which items are placed along a familiar route (a memory palace) and recalled by walking it in the mind; it is a way to improve memory, not a kind of forgetting.", "b": "The forgetting curve of Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885) plots how quickly memory for nonsense syllables is lost over time (steeply at first, then levelling off); it describes how retention falls as time passes, not one song intruding on another.", "d": "Retroactive interference runs the other way: newer learning disrupts recall of older learning. It would fit only if raag kaafi kept intruding when he tried to sing raag malkaus."}}, {"q": "Maslow's Need Hierarchy Model is broadly divided into which two categories of needs", "concept": "The answer is c: the needs in the hierarchy of Abraham Maslow (1943) fall into two broad categories, deficiency needs and growth needs. Maslow first set out five levels, from the bottom up: physiological needs (food, water, sleep), safety, belongingness and love, esteem, and self-actualisation (becoming all that one is capable of becoming). In Toward a Psychology of Being (1962) he sorted them into two kinds. Deficiency needs, or D-needs, are the four lower levels. They arise from a lack, like a hole that must be filled: the lack creates tension, and once the need is met it stops motivating. Growth needs, or being needs (B-needs), sit at the top, with self-actualisation as the main one. They do not come from a lack, and they grow stronger as they are satisfied: a person who paints for the joy of it wants to paint more, not less. Maslow later added self-transcendence at the very top, and extended versions of the pyramid count cognitive and aesthetic needs as growth needs too. Example: Arjun, newly arrived in Pune for work, first worries about rent, safety and making friends (D-needs); years later, settled and respected, he learns the sitar purely for the joy of mastering it (a B-need). Option c is right because deficiency versus growth (being) is Maslow's own two-way division of his hierarchy. Exam trap: option b looks plausible because Maslow did write about cognitive needs, but they are a kind of higher, growth need, not one of the two main categories; and \"all of the above\" fails because only c is Maslow's split.", "traps": {"a": "Material versus emotional needs is an everyday split, not Maslow's; his deficiency needs already mix bodily needs (food, safety) with emotional ones (love, esteem), so the line he drew is between lack and growth, not between matter and feeling.", "b": "Needs to know and understand do appear in Maslow's writing, but as one kind of higher (growth) need; cognitive versus emotional is not the two-way division of the hierarchy.", "d": "'All of the above' fails because a and b are not Maslow's categories; only c names his division into deficiency (D) and growth (B) needs."}}, {"q": "What is an ego defense mechanism?", "concept": "The answer is b: an ego defence mechanism is an unconscious process that keeps unacceptable id impulses under control, holding them out of awareness or disguising them so that the person is spared anxiety. Sigmund Freud (1894) first wrote about defence in \"The Neuro-Psychoses of Defence\", and his daughter Anna Freud (1936) catalogued the mechanisms in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence. The ego must balance the pleasure-seeking id, the moralising superego and reality; when a forbidden wish threatens to break through, the ego feels anxiety as a danger signal and responds with a defence. All defences share two features: they operate unconsciously, and they deny or distort reality. Repression, the basic defence, pushes the wish out of awareness. Others disguise it: projection (seeing one's own impulse in others), reaction formation (showing the opposite feeling), displacement (redirecting the impulse to a safer target), sublimation (channelling it into a socially valued activity), rationalisation, denial and regression. Example: Kabir is furious with his strict boss but cannot admit it even to himself; he comes home and snaps at his younger brother over nothing. The anger has been displaced, and Kabir honestly does not see the link. Option b is right because it is the only option that names the job of a defence, controlling unacceptable id impulses, and it places defences outside awareness. Strictly, defences are unconscious operations of the ego, so \"part of unconscious\" is loose wording, but its function is right. Exam trap: options a, c and d use the phrase \"ego state\", which sounds Freudian but is best known from the transactional analysis of Eric Berne (1961) (Parent, Adult, Child); none of them describes what a defence actually does.", "traps": {"a": "Defences do distort reality, but preventing rational thinking is not their purpose; their purpose is to reduce anxiety, and one of them, rationalisation, actually dresses a motive up in reasonable-sounding logic.", "c": "This reverses the idea: defences raise barriers (repression keeps a wish out of awareness) rather than reducing them, so 'designed to reduce barriers' describes no Freudian concept.", "d": "Some mature defences, such as sublimation, can be adaptive, but defences are not a mechanism of development; their defining job is to protect the ego from anxiety, not to aid growth."}}, {"q": "Learning by observing behaviour of others and noting consequences of that behaviour is known as", "concept": "The answer is a: learning by watching another person's behaviour and noting what happens to them is vicarious learning, also called observational learning or modelling. \"Vicarious\" means experienced through someone else. Albert Bandura (1965) showed that people can learn a behaviour without performing it and without being rewarded themselves. In the earlier Bobo doll study (Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross, 1961), nursery-school children who watched an adult punch and kick an inflatable doll later copied those very acts. In the 1965 study, children saw the model rewarded, punished or given no consequence. Those who saw the model punished imitated less, an effect called vicarious punishment, while seeing the model rewarded (vicarious reinforcement) kept imitation high. Yet when all the children were then offered treats for copying the model, the groups did about equally well. They had all learnt the behaviour; the consequences had changed performance, not learning. In Social Learning Theory (1977) Bandura named four processes: attention (noticing the model), retention (remembering what was done), reproduction (being able to do it) and motivation (having a reason to do it). Example: Meera sees a classmate scolded and given zero marks for copying in a test, and she decides never to copy herself, though she has never been caught. Option a is right because the stem describes both parts of vicarious learning: observing a model and noting the consequences the model receives. Exam trap: the other three options are sound-alike words starting with \"v\", placed to catch a student who half-remembers the term; only \"vicarious\" is a psychological term.", "traps": {"b": "'Vicious learning' is not a psychological term; it is there only because it sounds like 'vicarious'.", "c": "'Ventral learning' is not a psychological term; 'ventral' is an anatomical direction meaning towards the belly or front, and it is there only because it sounds like the answer.", "d": "'Vexed learning' is not a psychological term; 'vexed' simply means annoyed, and it is there only because it sounds like the answer."}}, {"q": "According to Cannon-Bard theory:", "concept": "The answer is a: in the Cannon-Bard theory the thalamus plays the central role in producing emotion. William James (1884) and Carl Lange (1885) had argued that bodily change comes first and the feeling is our perception of it: we are afraid because we tremble. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked this view. He argued that animals whose viscera were cut off from the brain still showed emotion, that much the same arousal goes with fear, anger and even fever, that the viscera are too slow to produce feelings that arise within a second, and that injected adrenaline produces arousal without true emotion. His alternative, which Philip Bard (1928) extended through experiments on rage in cats, was that an emotional stimulus is relayed by the thalamus, which sends signals at the same moment in two directions: up to the cortex, producing the conscious feeling, and down to the body through the sympathetic nervous system, producing arousal. Feeling and bodily change therefore happen together and independently; neither causes the other. Example: Riya hears a loud crack on a dark road, and her fear and her pounding heart arrive at the same instant. Option a is right because the thalamus, as the centre that triggers feeling and arousal in parallel, is the heart of the theory. Exam trap: option c borrows the word \"simultaneously\" to catch students who remember only that the theory is about simultaneous events; the simultaneity is between felt emotion and bodily arousal, not between two cortical lobes. Key note: Bard's own lesion work pointed to the hypothalamus as the trigger for the bodily side of rage, and later research moved the emotional centre to the limbic system; but the theory as taught names the thalamus, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "The cerebellum coordinates balance, posture and smooth movement; neither Walter Cannon nor Philip Bard gave it any role in emotion, and no theory makes it a gatekeeper for feelings.", "c": "The occipital lobe handles vision and the parietal lobe touch and spatial sense; this option borrows the theory's idea of simultaneity but puts it in the wrong place, since the theory's simultaneous events are felt emotion and bodily arousal, both triggered from the thalamus.", "d": "The sympathetic and parasympathetic branches of the autonomic nervous system usually act in opposition (arousing versus calming), not in concert; Walter Cannon did describe sympathetic fight-or-flight arousal, but the theory's central claim is about the thalamus, not autonomic balance."}}, {"q": "The tendency for distributed study or practice to yield better long-term retention than is achieved through massed study or practice is called?", "concept": "The answer is b: the advantage of spreading study over several sessions instead of cramming it into one is called the spacing effect, also known as the distributed practice effect. Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885), testing himself on lists of nonsense syllables, first noticed that repetitions spread over several days gave better retention than the same number of repetitions packed into one sitting. The effect has since been found with words, facts, motor skills and classroom material, in children and adults; Nicholas Cepeda and colleagues (2006) pooled hundreds of experiments and confirmed that spaced practice beats massed practice for long-term recall. One likely reason is that each spaced session forces effortful retrieval of partly forgotten material, which strengthens it; another is that the material is encoded in more varied contexts. Example: Kabir and Meera each give six hours to the nervous system chapter. Kabir does all six the night before the test; Meera does one hour a day for six days. Both may score well the next morning, but a month later Meera remembers far more. Option b is right because the stem is the textbook definition of the spacing effect: distributed practice beats massed practice for long-term retention. Exam trap: the other three options all belong to the serial position effect, the finding that in a list the first items (primacy) and the last items (recency) are recalled best (Bennet Murdock, 1962). Primacy reflects extra rehearsal into long-term memory; recency reflects items still held in short-term memory, and Murray Glanzer and Anita Cunitz (1966) showed it vanishes after a 30-second distracting delay. These effects concern an item's position in a list, not how practice is timed.", "traps": {"a": "The serial position effect (correctly named, not 'serial positioning') is the U-shaped curve of list recall, with the first and last items remembered best (Bennet Murdock, 1962); it concerns where an item sits in a list, not how study is spaced.", "c": "The recency effect is better recall of the last few items in a list, which are still in short-term memory; it is one end of the serial position curve, not the benefit of distributed practice.", "d": "The primacy effect is better recall of the first few items in a list, which get more rehearsal and so reach long-term memory; it is the other end of the serial position curve, not the spacing effect."}}, {"q": "Two train lines, although parallel to each other, still appear to converge at the end. This is an example of which kind of monocular cues?", "concept": "The answer is a: parallel railway lines that seem to meet in the distance show linear perspective, a monocular depth cue. Depth cues are the pieces of information the brain uses to build a three-dimensional world from the flat images on the retina. Monocular cues work with one eye, and most are pictorial, which is why artists can use them to make a flat canvas look deep. In linear perspective, parallel lines appear to converge as they recede, because the gap between them takes up a smaller and smaller image on the retina. Other monocular cues are relative size (the smaller of two similar objects looks farther away), interposition (a near object hides part of a far one), texture gradient (texture looks finer and denser with distance; James Gibson, 1950), aerial or atmospheric perspective (distant objects look hazy and bluish), shading, height in the visual field and motion parallax. Binocular cues need both eyes: retinal disparity (each eye receives a slightly different view, the principle of the stereoscope of Charles Wheatstone, 1838) and convergence (the eyes turn inwards to look at something near). Example: standing on the platform at Howrah, Arjun sees the two rails narrow to a single point at the horizon, though he knows they stay the same distance apart all the way. Option a is right because converging parallel lines are the defining example of linear perspective. Exam trap: the stem itself says the lines \"converge\", which tempts students to pick \"convergence\"; but convergence is the inward turning of the two eyes, a binocular cue, so it cannot answer a question about monocular cues.", "traps": {"b": "Convergence is a binocular cue: the eyes rotate inwards to fix on a near object, and the brain reads the muscle strain as nearness. It is not a monocular cue at all, and it concerns the eyes, not lines in the scene.", "c": "Aerial (atmospheric) perspective is a monocular cue in which distant objects, such as far hills, look hazy and bluish because of dust and moisture in the air; it works through clarity and colour, not converging lines.", "d": "Motion parallax is a monocular cue that needs movement: as you travel, near objects seem to rush past while far ones drift slowly. Standing and looking along the rails gives no parallax."}}, {"q": "Weber's Law, well established in perceptual research, states that:", "concept": "The answer is c: Weber's law states that a stimulus must be increased by a constant fraction of its value to be just noticeably different. Ernst Weber (1834), a German physiologist, asked people to compare lifted weights and found that the smallest change they could detect, the just noticeable difference (JND) or difference threshold, was not a fixed amount. It grew in proportion to the starting weight. In symbols, delta I divided by I equals k, where I is the starting intensity, delta I the change needed for a JND, and k a constant called the Weber fraction. Each sense has its own fraction, and a smaller fraction means finer discrimination. The law holds well across the middle range of intensities but breaks down for very weak and very strong stimuli. Example: Meera is lighting diyas for Diwali. With 10 lit, one more makes the room visibly brighter. With 100 lit, one more makes no visible difference; she needs about 10 more, because the ratio (1 in 10) must stay the same. Gustav Fechner (1860), in Elements of Psychophysics, built on Weber's finding to state that sensation grows with the logarithm of stimulus intensity (Fechner's law), and Stanley Smith Stevens (1957) later proposed a power law, in which each sense has its own exponent. Option c is right because \"a constant fraction of its value\" is exactly Weber's statement. Exam trap: option a, an equal absolute increase, is the opposite of Weber's law, because it ignores the starting level; also keep Weber (the JND is a constant proportion) apart from Fechner (sensation follows a logarithmic scale) and Stevens (a power function).", "traps": {"a": "An equal absolute increase at every level is the opposite of Weber's law; it would mean that adding one diya to 100 is as noticeable as adding one to 10, which is not what people perceive.", "b": "Doubling the stimulus is a fixed multiple that no psychophysical law proposes; real Weber fractions are much smaller than the whole stimulus and differ from one sense to another.", "d": "Ten times the amount is likewise a fixed multiple that no law proposes; Weber's point is that the change needed is a constant proportion of the starting value, usually a small one."}}, {"q": "The ability to focus on multiple things at the same time is:", "concept": "The answer is b: the ability to attend to two or more things at the same time is divided attention. Attention is the selection of some information for further processing, and its capacity is limited. Daniel Kahneman (1973), in Attention and Effort, described attention as a limited pool of mental resources shared among tasks: two tasks can run together only if their combined demand fits within the pool. Dual-task studies show that performance on both tasks usually drops when they are done together, unless one has become automatic through practice or the two use different channels, such as one visual and one auditory. The clinical model of McKay Moore Sohlberg and Catherine Mateer (1987), widely used in neuropsychological rehabilitation, separates five levels: focused attention (responding to a stimulus at all), sustained attention or vigilance (keeping attention over time), selective attention (attending to one input while ignoring others, as in the cocktail party effect studied by Colin Cherry, 1953), alternating attention (shifting back and forth between tasks) and divided attention (handling two tasks at once). Example: Riya rides her scooter through Delhi traffic while chatting with the friend sitting behind her. Her attention is divided across both tasks, and when the traffic suddenly thickens, the chat is the first thing to suffer. Option b is right because doing several things simultaneously is the definition of divided attention. Exam trap: alternating attention also involves more than one task, but the person switches between them one at a time; divided attention means handling them at the very same moment.", "traps": {"a": "Sustained attention (vigilance) is holding attention on one task over a long period, as a radar operator or a student in a three-hour exam must; it is about duration, not about several tasks at once.", "c": "Selective attention is focusing on one input while filtering out others, as in the cocktail party effect (Colin Cherry, 1953); it is the opposite of spreading attention across several things.", "d": "Alternating attention is switching back and forth between tasks with different demands, one at a time, as when a cook turns from chopping to stirring; the tasks are not handled at the same moment."}}, {"q": "According to Jean Piaget's cognitive development model, at which stage children are able to conserve, reverse their thinking and classify objects in terms of their many characteristic?", "concept": "The answer is c: conservation, reversible thinking and classifying objects by several features at once first appear in the concrete operational stage, from about 7 to 11 years. Jean Piaget (1936) held that children build knowledge through schemes that adapt by assimilation and accommodation, and he described four stages. In the sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2) the infant knows the world through action and achieves object permanence. In the preoperational stage (about 2 to 7) the child uses symbols and language but is egocentric and centres on one feature at a time, so fails conservation. In the concrete operational stage the child acquires operations, mental actions that can be reversed. This brings conservation (knowing that a quantity stays the same when only its shape or arrangement changes), reversibility (mentally undoing a change), decentration (attending to several features at once), classification, including class inclusion (there are more flowers than roses), and seriation (ordering sticks by length). The limit is that this logic works on concrete, real things, not abstract hypotheses; that comes in the formal operational stage (about 11 onwards). Example: water from a short, wide glass is poured into a tall, thin one. Five-year-old Arjun says the tall glass has more; eight-year-old Meera says it is the same water, because you could pour it back. Option c is right because conservation, reversibility and multiple classification are the hallmarks of concrete operations. Exam trap: the preoperational stage is the near-miss, because conservation tasks are usually taught under it, but there the child fails them; passing them marks the concrete operational stage.", "traps": {"a": "In the sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2) infants think through their senses and actions and achieve object permanence; they have no mental operations yet, so conservation is far beyond them.", "b": "In the preoperational stage (about 2 to 7) children use symbols but show centration, irreversibility and egocentrism, which is exactly why they fail conservation and class-inclusion tasks.", "d": "In the formal operational stage (about 11 onwards) adolescents keep concrete operations but add abstract, hypothetico-deductive reasoning; conservation and classification first appear earlier, in the concrete stage."}}, {"q": "Who among the following believed that development occurred as a maturational (or biological) process regardless of practice or training.", "concept": "The answer is b: Arnold Gesell (1925) held that development is a maturational, biological process that unfolds on its own timetable, largely regardless of practice or training. Gesell, a paediatrician and psychologist at Yale, observed and filmed thousands of infants and children and published age norms for motor, adaptive, language and personal-social behaviour, which became the Gesell Developmental Schedules. Maturation is growth that follows a genetic plan, so all children pass through the same sequence, though each at its own pace. His best-known evidence is the co-twin control study with Helen Thompson (1929). The two girls were identical twins, so heredity was held constant. Twin T was given daily practice in stair climbing for six weeks from the age of 46 weeks; twin C had no training until 53 weeks, yet after only about two weeks of practice she had caught up. Training before the body was ready had gained little. Gesell's advice to parents followed: watch for readiness instead of forcing skills. Example: Kabir's parents worry that he is not walking at ten months and plan daily walking drills. A Gesell-style answer is that he will walk when his nervous system and muscles are ready, and drills will hardly speed that up. Option b is right because Gesell is the textbook representative of the maturational view, in which nature sets the pace. Exam trap: Jean Piaget (1936) also used biological ideas such as adaptation, which makes him tempting, but he stressed the child's active interaction with the environment; John Watson (1913) is the environmentalist extreme, the very opposite of Gesell.", "traps": {"a": "Sigmund Freud (1905) did tie his psychosexual stages to biological drives, but he stressed that early experiences with parents shape personality for life, so he did not hold that development unfolds regardless of experience.", "c": "John Watson (1913), founder of behaviourism, was the extreme environmentalist who claimed he could train any healthy infant to become any kind of specialist; that is the opposite of a maturational view.", "d": "Jean Piaget (1936) saw development as the child actively constructing knowledge through interaction with the environment (assimilation, accommodation, equilibration), so experience is essential in his theory, not irrelevant."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about Erik Erikson's theory of development", "concept": "The answer is c: a defining feature of the theory of Erik Erikson (1950) is that it describes development across the entire life span. Erikson, trained as a psychoanalyst in Vienna, set out his psychosocial theory in Childhood and Society. He kept the idea of stages from Sigmund Freud (1905) but made two big changes. First, he shifted the focus from sexual instincts to social and cultural relationships and the growing ego, so his stages are psychosocial, not psychosexual. Second, he carried development beyond adolescence, through adulthood and into old age. He proposed eight stages, each built around a crisis between two poles: trust versus mistrust (infancy), autonomy versus shame and doubt (toddlerhood), initiative versus guilt (preschool years), industry versus inferiority (school age), identity versus role confusion (adolescence), intimacy versus isolation (young adulthood), generativity versus stagnation (middle adulthood) and integrity versus despair (late adulthood). A good resolution of each crisis yields a strength, such as hope in infancy and wisdom in old age. Example: Meera, aged 45, trains young nurses and feels her work will outlive her, which is generativity; her grandfather, aged 78, looks back on his life with acceptance, which is integrity. Option c is right because covering the whole life span, from birth to death, is what set Erikson's theory apart from Freud's and Piaget's, whose final stages begin in adolescence and add nothing for adulthood. Exam trap: Erikson was a neo-Freudian ego psychologist, not a neo-Piagetian; examiners also swap his \"psychosocial\" with Freud's \"psychosexual\".", "traps": {"a": "Neo-Piagetians, such as Robbie Case and Juan Pascual-Leone, revised Jean Piaget's cognitive stages using information-processing ideas; Erikson came from psychoanalysis, which makes him a neo-Freudian, and he studied psychosocial rather than cognitive growth.", "b": "Seeing people as products of their sexual instincts describes Sigmund Freud's psychosexual theory; Erikson deliberately moved the emphasis from sexual drives to social and cultural forces.", "d": "'None of the above' fails because statement c is true: the life-span scope is the most widely cited feature of Erikson's theory."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is the correct order of Kohlberg's theory of self-socialization:", "concept": "The answer is c: in the account of Lawrence Kohlberg (1966), children move from gender labelling to gender stability to gender consistency. Kohlberg offered a cognitive-developmental theory of gender. Its central claim is that understanding comes first: once a child grasps that she is a girl, she actively seeks out and copies what girls do, so she socialises herself. This reverses social learning theory, in which rewards and models shape gender behaviour before any understanding. Gender constancy, the full understanding that one's sex is permanent, develops in three steps. Gender labelling, or basic gender identity (about 2 to 3 years): the child can correctly say \"I am a girl\" and label others. Gender stability (about 3 to 5): she knows gender stays the same over time, so a boy grows up to be a man. Gender consistency (about 5 to 7): she knows gender stays the same across situations and appearances, so a boy in a frock or with long hair is still a boy. Ronald Slaby and Karin Frey (1975) supported the theory: children further along in constancy paid more attention to same-sex adults in a film. A criticism is that children prefer gender-typed toys by about age 2, well before constancy. Example: Riya, aged 3, says \"I am a girl\"; at 4 she says she will be an amma when she grows up; at 6 she insists she is still a girl even after her hair is cut short like her brother's. Option c is right because labelling, then stability, then consistency is the order Kohlberg proposed, each step building on the one before. Exam trap: the options swap stability and consistency; stability (over time) always comes before consistency (across situations), and gender schema theory (Sandra Bem, 1981) is a different, later theory.", "traps": {"a": "This is the full sequence in reverse: consistency is the final achievement, at about 5 to 7, and labelling is the first, at about 2 to 3, so consistency cannot come first.", "b": "Stability cannot come before labelling: a child must first be able to call herself a girl before she can understand that she will stay a girl over time.", "d": "This swaps the last two steps: stability (gender stays the same over time) comes before consistency (gender stays the same despite changes in clothes, hair or activities)."}}, {"q": "According to the preconventional morality phase in Kohlberg's theory:", "concept": "The answer is a: at the preconventional level, right and wrong are judged by the hedonistic consequences of an action, the reward or punishment it brings to oneself. Lawrence Kohlberg (1958) studied moral reasoning by presenting dilemmas, the best known being the Heinz dilemma, in which a poor man must decide whether to steal an expensive drug to save his dying wife. He scored the reasons people gave, not the choice they made, and found three levels with two stages each. At the preconventional level, typical of children under about 9, morality is controlled from outside. Stage 1, the punishment and obedience orientation, calls an act wrong if it gets punished. Stage 2, the instrumental or individualism and exchange orientation, calls right whatever serves one's own needs, including fair deals of the \"I help you, you help me\" kind. At the conventional level, rules are followed to win others' approval (stage 3, good boy and good girl) and to maintain law and social order (stage 4). At the postconventional level, laws are seen as social contracts that can be changed for the greater good (stage 5), and finally as resting on universal ethical principles (stage 6). Example: asked why Heinz should not steal, Riya, aged 6, says \"because the police will catch him\"; asked why she helps her brother with homework, she says \"so he gives me his chocolate\". Both answers rest on consequences to herself. Option a is right because \"hedonistic consequences\", gaining pleasure and avoiding pain, is exactly the preconventional basis of judgement. Exam trap: option b describes the conventional level and option c the postconventional level; examiners shuffle the levels, so remember that \"pre\" means before society's conventions.", "traps": {"b": "Morality based on social rules, approval and law and order is Kohlberg's conventional level (stages 3 and 4), which usually develops in late childhood and adolescence.", "c": "Seeing moral rules as social conventions that serve the greater good is the postconventional level, stage 5 (the social contract orientation), which Kohlberg found in only a minority of adults.", "d": "'None of the above' fails because option a is the standard textbook description of preconventional morality."}}, {"q": "Contact comfort is more essential in bond formation than providing for physical needs was concluded by:", "concept": "The answer is d: Harry Harlow (1958) concluded that contact comfort matters more for forming an attachment bond than meeting physical needs such as feeding. Before his work, both psychoanalysis and behaviourism held a \"cupboard love\" view: infants become attached to the mother because she feeds them and so reduces their hunger drive. Harlow tested this at the University of Wisconsin with infant rhesus monkeys separated from their mothers soon after birth. Each was raised with two surrogate mothers, one made of bare wire mesh and one covered in soft terry cloth. For some monkeys only the wire mother held a milk bottle. These monkeys went to the wire mother to feed but spent most of their time clinging to the cloth mother. When a frightening mechanical toy was put into the cage, they ran to the cloth mother and, once calmed, used her as a safe base from which to explore. Harlow reported this in \"The Nature of Love\". Later work showed that monkeys reared with surrogates alone grew up socially and sexually abnormal, so contact comfort is needed but not sufficient. Example: baby Kabir takes his bottle from a nanny, but when a firecracker bursts outside he reaches for his mother, who holds and rocks him every evening. Option d is right because Harlow's surrogate mother experiments are the classic evidence that comfort, not food, drives attachment. Exam trap: John Bowlby (1969) also rejected cupboard love and cited Harlow's monkeys, so he is the near-miss; Bowlby built attachment theory, but Harlow ran the contact comfort studies. Key note: the printed key table in Anukriti's solved copy gives a (John Bowlby), but her own worked solution names Harry Harlow, and the official Gurugram key for the same question is Harlow; the table letter is a slip, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "John Bowlby (1969) built attachment theory, arguing that attachment is an evolved system that keeps infants close to a caregiver; he drew on Harlow's monkeys as evidence but did not run the contact comfort experiments.", "b": "Jerome Kagan is known for research on temperament, especially behavioural inhibition, the shy and wary style seen in some infants; he did not study contact comfort.", "c": "Mary Ainsworth (1978) devised the Strange Situation and described secure, avoidant and resistant attachment in human infants; she classified the quality of attachment rather than showing that comfort outweighs feeding."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a part of fore brain", "concept": "The answer is b: the cerebellum is not part of the forebrain; it belongs to the hindbrain. In the embryo the front of the neural tube swells into three primary regions, which give the brain its three main divisions. The forebrain (prosencephalon) is the largest. It divides into the telencephalon, which forms the cerebrum (the two cerebral hemispheres with their cortex), the basal ganglia and most limbic structures, and the diencephalon, which forms the thalamus and hypothalamus. The midbrain (mesencephalon) contains the tectum, with the superior colliculi for vision and the inferior colliculi for hearing, and the tegmentum, which includes the substantia nigra. The hindbrain (rhombencephalon) divides into the metencephalon, which forms the pons and the cerebellum, and the myelencephalon, which forms the medulla oblongata. The cerebellum, the \"little brain\" tucked behind the brainstem, coordinates balance, posture, the timing of movements and motor learning. Damage to it causes ataxia, an unsteady, drunken-looking walk and clumsy reaching. Example: Arjun's uncle has a small stroke in the cerebellum; his thinking and memory are intact, but he sways when he walks and misses the glass when he reaches for it. Option b is right because the cerebellum develops from the hindbrain, along with the pons and medulla. Exam trap: \"cerebrum\" and \"cerebellum\" look alike, but the cerebrum is the crowning part of the forebrain while the cerebellum is hindbrain; also remember that the thalamus, though it sits deep in the middle of the brain, is forebrain (diencephalon).", "traps": {"a": "The cerebrum, the two cerebral hemispheres with their cortex, develops from the telencephalon and is the largest part of the forebrain, so it clearly belongs to it.", "c": "The thalamus, the main relay station for sensory signals on their way to the cortex, develops from the diencephalon, which is part of the forebrain, so it does belong to the forebrain.", "d": "The limbic system (hippocampus, amygdala, cingulate gyrus, septal nuclei and related structures) lies in the forebrain, mainly in the telencephalon with links to the diencephalon, so it does belong to the forebrain."}}, {"q": "What is the function of reticular formation", "concept": "The answer is a: the reticular formation controls arousal, alertness and attention. It is a net-like web of neurons (\"reticular\" means net-like) running through the core of the brainstem, from the medulla through the pons to the midbrain. Its ascending part, the reticular activating system, sends fibres up through the thalamus to the whole cortex and keeps it awake and responsive. It also acts as a filter, letting important or novel stimuli through and screening out routine background input. Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun (1949) showed its role: electrically stimulating the brainstem reticular formation in cats switched the EEG from the slow waves of drowsiness to the fast, low-voltage pattern of alertness. Damage to it can cause lasting coma. Its descending part helps regulate muscle tone, posture and some autonomic functions, but arousal and attention is its textbook role. Example: Meera sleeps through the steady hum of the ceiling fan, yet wakes at once when her baby whimpers, because her reticular formation lets the meaningful sound through and rouses the cortex. Option a is right because arousal, alertness and the filtering of attention are the classic functions of the reticular activating system. Exam trap: each wrong option names the function of a nearby structure: refined movement belongs to the cerebellum, emotion to the limbic system, and hunger to the hypothalamus.", "traps": {"b": "Refined, coordinated movement is the work of the cerebellum, together with the motor cortex and basal ganglia; the reticular formation's motor role is limited to posture and muscle tone.", "c": "Emotion is mainly the work of the limbic system, especially the amygdala, with the hypothalamus driving the body's emotional responses; the reticular formation sets general arousal, not specific emotions.", "d": "Hunger is regulated by the hypothalamus: damage to the lateral hypothalamus reduces eating, while damage to the ventromedial area leads to overeating; this is not a reticular function."}}, {"q": "Which of the following forms a part of the limbic system", "concept": "The answer is d: the septal nuclei, the amygdala and the hippocampus are all parts of the limbic system. The limbic system is a ring of interconnected structures on the inner border of each cerebral hemisphere, curving around the top of the brainstem (\"limbus\" is Latin for border). James Papez (1937) proposed a circuit for emotion, and Paul MacLean (1952) named the wider network the limbic system. Its main parts are the hippocampus, the amygdala, the septal nuclei, the cingulate gyrus, the fornix and the mammillary bodies, with the hypothalamus and parts of the thalamus often included. The hippocampus consolidates new memories: after Henry Molaison (patient H.M.) had large parts of both hippocampi and the nearby medial temporal tissue removed in 1953, he could no longer form new lasting memories of facts and events. The amygdala detects threat and gives memories their emotional weight; removing the temporal lobes, amygdala included, in monkeys produced the tameness and loss of fear described by Heinrich Kluver and Paul Bucy (1939). The septal area is linked with pleasure and reward: James Olds and Peter Milner (1954) found that rats would press a lever again and again to stimulate electrodes placed there. Example: Kabir freezes when he sees a dog like the one that once bit him (amygdala) and can recall exactly where the bite happened (hippocampus). Option d is right because all three named structures are core limbic components. Exam trap: in an \"all of the above\" item, students often stop at the first famous structure, the amygdala or hippocampus, and miss that the less familiar septal nuclei also belong to the limbic system.", "traps": {"a": "The septal nuclei are indeed a limbic structure linked with pleasure and reward, but choosing a alone leaves out the amygdala and hippocampus, which are limbic too.", "b": "The amygdala is indeed a limbic structure for fear and emotional memory, but it is only one of the three correct parts listed, so a single-letter answer is incomplete.", "c": "The hippocampus is indeed a limbic structure for forming new memories, but the septal nuclei and amygdala also belong to the limbic system, so c alone is incomplete."}}, {"q": "Which lobes is responsible for vision", "concept": "The answer is b: the occipital lobe, at the back of the brain, is responsible for vision. Each cerebral hemisphere has four lobes, marked off by landmarks such as the central sulcus and the lateral fissure. The occipital lobe holds the primary visual cortex (V1), also called the striate cortex or area 17 on the map of Korbinian Brodmann (1909), which lies along the calcarine sulcus on the inner surface of the lobe. Signals travel from the retina along the optic nerve and optic tract to the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus, and from there to V1. The left occipital lobe receives the right half of the visual field and the right lobe the left half. From V1, information flows to surrounding visual association areas and then along two streams: a ventral \"what\" stream into the temporal lobe for recognising objects and faces, and a dorsal \"where\" stream into the parietal lobe for location and action. Damage to V1 causes cortical blindness or loss of part of the visual field, even when the eyes are healthy. Example: after a fall that injures the back of her head, Riya's grandmother cannot see anything to her left, though her eye doctor finds both eyes normal; the damage is in her right occipital lobe. Option b is right because the primary visual cortex lies in the occipital lobe. Exam trap: the temporal lobe (hearing, language comprehension, face recognition) and the parietal lobe (touch, spatial attention) both take part in later stages of vision, which makes them tempting, but the primary visual area is occipital.", "traps": {"a": "The frontal lobe handles planning, decision making, voluntary movement (the primary motor cortex) and speech production (Broca's area); it directs eye movements but does not process what is seen.", "c": "The parietal lobe holds the primary somatosensory cortex for touch and body position and supports spatial attention; it receives the dorsal 'where' stream from vision, but the primary visual cortex is not there.", "d": "The temporal lobe holds the primary auditory cortex, Wernicke's area for understanding speech and areas for recognising faces and objects; it is the seat of hearing, not of primary vision."}}, {"q": "The part of the brain which connects the left hemisphere with the right hemisphere is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: the corpus callosum is the structure that connects the left and right cerebral hemispheres. It is a thick, arched band of white matter lying deep in the groove between the hemispheres, made of roughly 200 million axons. A bundle that links the two sides of the brain is called a commissure, and the corpus callosum is by far the largest; the much smaller anterior commissure also joins the hemispheres. Through it the two halves share information, so that what one side sees or feels the other soon knows. In severe epilepsy, surgeons sometimes cut it to stop seizures spreading from one hemisphere to the other. Studying these split-brain patients, Roger Sperry (1968), who won the Nobel Prize in 1981, and Michael Gazzaniga (1967) showed that each hemisphere has its own specialities. When a word was flashed to the right hemisphere only, patients could not name it, because speech is usually controlled by the left hemisphere, yet their left hand could pick out the matching object. Example: Arjun has had split-brain surgery. Holding a comb in his left hand out of sight, he cannot say what it is, but with the same hand he can pick out a comb from other objects. Option a is right because the corpus callosum is the main commissure joining the two hemispheres. Exam trap: Broca's area and Wernicke's area are both language areas within one hemisphere, usually the left, and the inferior colliculi lie in the midbrain; none of them connects the hemispheres.", "traps": {"b": "The inferior colliculi are paired bumps on the roof (tectum) of the midbrain that relay auditory signals towards the thalamus; they serve hearing, not the link between the cerebral hemispheres.", "c": "Wernicke's area, described by Carl Wernicke (1874), lies in the left temporal lobe and is needed to understand language; it is a region within one hemisphere, not a connection between the two.", "d": "Broca's area, described by Paul Broca (1861) from his patient 'Tan', lies in the left frontal lobe and controls speech production; it is a cortical area within one hemisphere, not a bridge between them."}}, {"q": "The function of myelin sheath is to:", "concept": "The answer is a: the myelin sheath insulates nerve fibres (axons) from one another and from the fluid around them. Myelin is a fatty, whitish covering wrapped in many tight layers around an axon, made by oligodendrocytes in the central nervous system and by Schwann cells in the peripheral nervous system. The sheath is not continuous: between the wrapped segments lie short bare gaps, the nodes of Ranvier, where sodium channels cluster. Because charge cannot leak out through the insulated stretches, current spreads quickly inside the axon to the next node, and the action potential is renewed only at the nodes. The impulse seems to leap from node to node: saltatory conduction (Latin saltare, to jump). The pay-off is speed: thin unmyelinated fibres conduct at about 1 metre per second, large myelinated ones at up to about 120 metres per second. Example: Meera, 28, develops blurred vision and tingling legs, and an MRI shows patches of lost myelin. This is multiple sclerosis, in which the immune system attacks central myelin, so impulses slow or fail. Option a is right because insulation is what the sheath itself physically does, and it is the means by which myelin speeds conduction. Exam trap: because the sheath wraps the axon, students pick 'carry neural message'; but the axon carries the impulse, and myelin only insulates it. Key note: the main purpose of myelin is faster conduction, and some papers key 'increases the speed of the impulse' instead; myelin gets that speed by insulating the axon, and among these four options only a names what myelin itself does, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Carrying the neural message is the job of the axon itself, the long fibre along which the action potential travels; myelin wraps the axon and speeds the message but does not carry it.", "c": "The energy centre of a neuron, as of any cell, is the mitochondrion, which makes ATP; myelin is a fatty insulating layer made by glial cells and produces no energy.", "d": "Receiving incoming information is the job of the dendrites and cell body, whose receptors pick up neurotransmitter from other neurons; myelin covers the axon, the sending part of the cell."}}, {"q": "Neural conduction within a neuron is:", "concept": "The answer is a: conduction within a neuron is an electrical process. Communication in the nervous system has two legs. The first runs inside a single cell: signals picked up by the dendrites and cell body are summed at the axon hillock, and if the membrane there reaches threshold (about -55 millivolts) the neuron fires an action potential. Sodium channels open and positive sodium ions pour in, then potassium ions flow out to restore the charge. This wave of voltage change sweeps along the axon to its terminals without losing strength, and it obeys the all-or-none law. Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) worked out these ionic steps by recording from the giant axon of the squid. The second leg runs between cells. At the terminal the voltage change triggers the release of a chemical neurotransmitter, which drifts across the synaptic cleft and acts on receptors of the next neuron. Otto Loewi (1921) demonstrated this chemical step: fluid from a stimulated frog heart slowed a second heart, so a substance (his 'Vagusstoff', later identified as acetylcholine) must carry the message. Hence the classroom summary: electrical within, chemical between. Example: when Kabir steps on a thorn, the signal races along his sensory axon as an electrical impulse and then crosses to a spinal cord neuron chemically. Option a is right because the stem says 'within a neuron', the electrical leg. Exam trap: 'Both a and b' tempts students who remember the word electrochemical; in that summary the word covers the whole chain, synapse included. Key note: the action potential itself is sometimes called an electrochemical event, because it is produced by ions moving across the membrane, so c has a case; but the standard teaching line is electrical within a neuron and chemical between neurons, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Chemical transmission is what happens between neurons, at the synapse, where neurotransmitters cross the synaptic cleft (Otto Loewi's frog-heart experiment); it is not how the impulse travels inside one neuron.", "c": "'Both' describes the full electrochemical chain from one neuron to the next, electrical along the axon and chemical across the synapse; the stem limits the question to conduction within a single neuron.", "d": "Conduction within a neuron is a well understood electrical event, the action potential, so 'none of the above' cannot be right."}}, {"q": "The electrical charge at resting potential across the neuron is", "concept": "The answer is a: the resting potential of a neuron is about -70 millivolts: the inside of the cell sits about 70 millivolts negative compared with the outside. A neuron that is not firing is said to be polarised. Textbooks round the figure to -70, though real neurons vary between roughly -60 and -80. Two features of the membrane produce it. First, ions are unevenly spread: sodium (Na+) is concentrated outside the cell, while potassium (K+) and large negative protein molecules are concentrated inside. Second, the resting membrane is leaky to potassium but almost closed to sodium, so potassium drifts out and leaves an excess of negative charge behind. The sodium-potassium pump, which uses energy, keeps the gradients steady by moving three sodium ions out for every two potassium ions in. If a stimulus nudges the membrane up to threshold (about -55 millivolts), sodium channels open and the voltage shoots up to a peak of about +30 to +40 millivolts, then falls back, briefly dips below rest (hyperpolarisation) and returns to -70. Example: Riya fixes it in memory as 'seventy, minus, inside': at rest the inside is 70 millivolts negative. Option a is right because -70 millivolts is the standard textbook value of the resting potential. Exam trap: +40 millivolts is the peak of the spike and -40 millivolts lies past threshold, so both are near-misses; and +70 has the sign the wrong way round.", "traps": {"b": "+70 millivolts reverses the sign: at rest the inside of the neuron is negative relative to the outside, never 70 millivolts positive.", "c": "-40 millivolts is a depolarised value, less negative than the usual threshold of about -55 millivolts, so a neuron there would be firing, not resting.", "d": "+40 millivolts is roughly the peak (overshoot) of the action potential, after sodium has rushed in, the opposite end of the cycle from rest."}}, {"q": "Neurotransmitters are present in the:", "concept": "The answer is c: neurotransmitters are held in synaptic vesicles, tiny membrane-bound sacs packed inside the axon terminals. A neurotransmitter is the chemical messenger that carries a signal across the synapse. Small-molecule transmitters such as acetylcholine and dopamine are made in the terminal and packed into vesicles there; larger neuropeptides are made in the cell body (soma) and carried down the axon. When an action potential reaches the terminal, calcium channels open, calcium flows in, and the vesicles fuse with the membrane and spill their contents into the synaptic cleft (exocytosis). The transmitter binds to receptors on the next neuron and is then cleared by reuptake or enzymes. In the early 1950s Bernard Katz and his colleagues showed that transmitter is released in fixed packets, or quanta, now understood as the contents of single vesicles; Katz shared the Nobel Prize in 1970. Example: when Kabir lifts his cricket bat, vesicles of acetylcholine in his motor neuron terminals release their load onto his arm muscles. Option c is right because the vesicles are the actual containers in which neurotransmitter is stored. Exam trap: a paper may ask where transmitters are released, and then 'axon terminals' is the answer; here the question asks where they are present, and the precise container is the vesicle. Key note: the vesicles sit inside the synaptic terminals, so b is not false; but the question asks where the transmitter is held, and the most precise answer is the vesicles, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "The soma (cell body) holds the nucleus and makes proteins, including the neuropeptide transmitters, but transmitters are stored in vesicles in the axon terminals, not kept in the soma.", "b": "The synaptic terminals (terminal buttons) are where the vesicles sit and where release happens, so b is close; but within the terminal the transmitter is held in the vesicles, the more exact answer.", "d": "The axon carries the action potential from the cell body to the terminals; neuropeptides are transported along it, but it is not where neurotransmitters are stored."}}, {"q": "Which of the following best defines Operational Definition", "concept": "The answer is a: an operational definition states how a researcher will measure (or produce) the variables in a study. Many psychological concepts, such as anxiety, intelligence or aggression, cannot be seen directly. An operational definition turns such a concept into something observable by naming the exact procedure, or operation, used to measure or manipulate it. Anxiety becomes the score on a named anxiety inventory; hunger becomes hours without food; aggression becomes the number of times a child hits a doll in ten minutes. The idea comes from the physicist Percy Bridgman (1927), whose operationism held that a concept means the set of operations used to measure it; Stanley Smith Stevens (1935) brought it into psychology. Operational definitions make research objective, replicable and testable; their limit is that one procedure captures only part of a construct. Example: Riya wants to study exam stress among Class 12 students in Jaipur. She defines exam stress as the score on a standard perceived stress scale taken one week before the board exams, plus resting heart rate measured that morning. Option a is right because an operational definition is exactly the recipe for measuring a variable. Exam trap: the distractors are definitions of neighbouring research terms placed side by side: a hypothesis (b), the first step of research (c) and a variable (d). A variable is the thing that varies; the operational definition is how you measure it.", "traps": {"b": "A testable explanation of the relationship between two variables is a hypothesis, which predicts a result; the operational definition only says how each variable in that hypothesis will be measured.", "c": "Identifying a problem to study is problem formulation, the first step of the research process, which comes before any variable is defined.", "d": "A factor that varies in amount and can be measured is the definition of a variable itself, not of the procedure used to measure it."}}, {"q": "When a relevant subgroups of the population are randomly sampled in proportion to its size, it is:", "concept": "The answer is b: when relevant subgroups of a population are each randomly sampled in proportion to their size, the method is stratified random sampling. The researcher first divides the population into strata: subgroups that do not overlap, that together include everyone, and that are formed on a variable likely to matter for the study, such as sex, year of study, region or type of school. A separate random sample is then drawn from every stratum. In proportionate stratified sampling each stratum gets the same sampling fraction, so the sample mirrors the population on that variable. In disproportionate stratified sampling small strata are deliberately over-sampled so that each can be analysed on its own, and results are weighted back for the whole. Jerzy Neyman (1934) worked out optimum allocation, which takes more cases from strata that are larger or more variable. Because no subgroup can be left out or over-represented by bad luck, estimates are usually more precise than with a simple random sample of the same size. Example: Meera's college has 600 women and 400 men. For a sample of 100, she randomly picks 60 names from the women's list and 40 from the men's list. Option b is right because the stem describes both features: random selection within subgroups, and selection in proportion to their size. Exam trap: cluster sampling also starts by dividing the population into groups, but it randomly picks whole groups (schools, villages) and studies everyone in them, while stratified sampling takes some members from every group.", "traps": {"a": "Simple random sampling gives every member of the population an equal chance of selection from one single list, without first dividing it into subgroups, so it cannot guarantee that each subgroup appears in proportion.", "c": "A representative sample is the goal, a sample that mirrors the population, not a technique; stratified random sampling is one way of reaching it.", "d": "Cluster sampling randomly selects whole natural groups, such as schools or villages, and studies all members of the chosen groups; the unit sampled is the group, and many groups are left out entirely."}}, {"q": "When we say that the population mean is same as the sample mean, it signifies:", "concept": "The answer is d: saying that the population mean is the same as the sample mean, so that there is no real difference, is the null hypothesis. A statistical test always pits two hypotheses against each other. The null hypothesis, written H0, is the 'nothing is going on' claim: no difference, no effect, no relationship. Here it says the sample comes from a population whose mean equals the value being compared, so any gap we see is just sampling error. The alternative hypothesis, H1, is the claim that a real difference exists. The term comes from Ronald Fisher (1935, The Design of Experiments): the null is the hypothesis the data may nullify. The logic runs backwards from what students expect. We assume H0 is true and ask how probable our result would be if it were. If that probability (the p value) is smaller than the alpha level, usually .05, we reject H0; if not, we fail to reject it, which is not the same as proving it. Example: Arjun asks whether the students at his coaching centre differ in average IQ from the population mean of 100. His H0: the centre's mean is 100. His H1: it is not 100. Option d is right because only the null hypothesis states equality, or no difference. Exam trap: research, alternative and experimental hypotheses all predict that a difference exists, the opposite of the stem; a statement of 'no difference' or 'equals' is the null. Key note: strictly, a null hypothesis is about population values (the population mean equals a stated value, or two population means are equal), not a claim that a sample mean equals the population mean, so the stem is loosely worded; it still describes 'no difference', so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "A research hypothesis is the researcher's prediction, drawn from theory, that a difference or relationship exists, such as 'yoga lowers anxiety'; it asserts a difference, not equality.", "b": "The alternative hypothesis (H1) is the statistical rival of the null: it states that the population mean differs from the value tested, and it is accepted only when the null is rejected.", "c": "An experimental hypothesis is the research hypothesis stated for an experiment, predicting that the independent variable will change the dependent variable; it too claims a difference."}}, {"q": "Type II error is made by the researcher when:", "concept": "The answer is c: a Type II error is made when the null hypothesis is false but the researcher accepts it (strictly, fails to reject it). Every significance test ends in a decision about the null hypothesis (H0, the claim of no effect), and the decision can be right or wrong. Retaining a true null and rejecting a false null are correct decisions. Rejecting a true null is a Type I error, a false positive: the researcher announces an effect that is not there. Its probability is alpha, fixed in advance, usually at .05. Retaining a false null is a Type II error, a false negative: a real effect goes undetected. Its probability is beta. The labels come from the framework of Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1928, 1933). Power, 1 minus beta, is the chance of catching a real effect; small samples and small effects lower it and so raise the risk of a Type II error. Example: Kabir tests whether a mindfulness course reduces exam anxiety. The course truly works, but with only 12 students the difference is not significant, and he concludes it has no effect: a Type II error. Option c is right because 'null false and accepted' is exactly a missed real effect. Exam trap: the two errors are easy to swap. Think of a smoke alarm: a Type I error is the alarm ringing with no fire; a Type II error is the alarm staying silent during a real fire.", "traps": {"a": "Rejecting a true null hypothesis is a Type I error (alpha, a false positive): the researcher claims an effect that does not exist.", "b": "Accepting (retaining) a true null hypothesis is a correct decision, not an error; its probability is 1 minus alpha.", "d": "Rejecting a false null hypothesis is also a correct decision; its probability is the power of the test, 1 minus beta."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a type of construct validity", "concept": "The answer is a: convergent validity is one of the main forms of evidence for construct validity. A construct is a trait that cannot be seen directly but is inferred from behaviour, such as anxiety, intelligence or extraversion. Construct validity asks whether a test truly measures that construct, and Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) argued that it is shown by checking whether scores relate to other things the way the theory predicts. Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959) made this concrete in the multitrait-multimethod matrix, which separates two kinds of evidence. Convergent validity: the test should correlate highly with other measures of the same construct, even ones using a different method (a self-report anxiety scale should agree with a clinician's anxiety rating). Discriminant, or divergent, validity: it should correlate weakly with measures of unrelated constructs (the anxiety scale should not track vocabulary). Factor analysis and expected group differences add further construct evidence. In the classic three-way scheme, the other two types are content validity and criterion-related validity, the latter split into concurrent and predictive. Example: Kabir builds a Hindi anxiety scale for adults. It correlates 0.70 with an established anxiety inventory (convergent evidence) and 0.05 with an arithmetic test (discriminant evidence). Option a is right because agreement with other measures of the same construct is, by definition, construct evidence. Exam trap: concurrent validity sounds almost like convergent validity, but it is a subtype of criterion validity, in which test scores are compared with a criterion measured at the same time, such as a current diagnosis.", "traps": {"b": "Criterion-related validity is its own category in the classic trio: test scores are compared with an external standard such as grades, job ratings or a diagnosis, rather than with other measures of the same theoretical trait.", "c": "Content validity is also its own category: experts judge whether the items cover the whole domain the test is meant to sample, as an exam should cover the full syllabus.", "d": "Concurrent validity is a form of criterion validity in which the test and the criterion are measured at about the same time; it is offered because it sounds like 'convergent'."}}, {"q": "In which measurement scale can we use all arithmetic operation i.e. addition, subtraction, multiplication and division", "concept": "The answer is c: addition, subtraction, multiplication and division are all meaningful only on a ratio scale. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels, each adding one property. A nominal scale only names categories (blood group, a cricketer's shirt number); you can count cases and find the mode, but no arithmetic makes sense. An ordinal scale adds order (class rank, a five-point agreement rating); first is above second, but not by a known amount, so the median and percentiles suit it. An interval scale adds equal units, so differences mean the same everywhere (temperature in Celsius, and by convention IQ scores); you can add and subtract and compute a mean and standard deviation. But its zero is arbitrary, so ratios mislead: 20 degrees Celsius is not twice as hot as 10. A ratio scale adds a true zero that means none of the quantity at all (height, weight, reaction time, income, temperature in kelvin). Because zero is real, multiplication and division work too: a 20 kilogram bag really is twice as heavy as a 10 kilogram one. Example: Meera times students solving a puzzle. Arjun takes 30 seconds and Riya 60, so Riya took twice as long, a valid statement because time has a true zero. Option c is right because only the ratio scale supports meaningful multiplication and division as well as addition and subtraction. Exam trap: the interval scale also has equal units and allows addition and subtraction, so it tempts; without a true zero it cannot support ratios.", "traps": {"a": "A nominal scale only labels categories (blood group, gender); its numbers are names, so no arithmetic, not even addition, is meaningful.", "b": "An interval scale (Celsius temperature, IQ) has equal units, so addition and subtraction work, but its zero is arbitrary, so multiplication and division give meaningless ratios.", "d": "An ordinal scale (class rank) shows only order; the gaps between ranks are unequal, so even adding and subtracting the numbers is not meaningful."}}, {"q": "All else being equal, which of the following signifies strongest correlation", "concept": "The answer is a: -0.96 is the strongest correlation among the possible values offered. A correlation coefficient, such as the product-moment r of Karl Pearson (1896), carries two separate pieces of information. Its sign shows the direction: positive means the two variables rise together, negative means one rises as the other falls. Its size, read without the sign (the absolute value), shows the strength: 0 means no straight-line relationship and 1 means a perfect one, with every point on a straight line. So r always lies between -1 and +1. Comparing by size, -0.96 is 0.96 away from zero, +0.85 is 0.85 and -0.24 only 0.24. The value 1.05 is impossible, because no correlation can go beyond +1 or -1. Squaring r gives the shared variance: 0.96 squared is about 0.92, so the two variables share about 92 percent of their variance, against about 72 percent for 0.85 and under 6 percent for 0.24. Example: Kabir finds that the hours his classmates spend gaming on weeknights correlate -0.96 with their attendance: the more they game, the less they attend, and the points lie almost on a downward line. Option a is right because strength is judged by distance from zero, not by sign, and -0.96 is the farthest from zero of the valid values. Exam trap: students treat a minus sign as weaker and pick +0.85, or pick 1.05 because it is the biggest number; the sign shows direction only, and no r can exceed 1.", "traps": {"b": "-0.24 is a weak negative correlation, only 0.24 away from zero, so the two variables share under 6 percent of their variance.", "c": "+0.85 is a strong positive correlation, but its size, 0.85, is smaller than the 0.96 of option a; the plus sign does not make it stronger.", "d": "1.05 is not a possible value of a correlation coefficient, which cannot exceed +1 or fall below -1, so it signals an error, not a very strong relationship."}}, {"q": "The design which concerns the question of how many times items are to be observed and how the information and data gathered is to be analysed is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: the part of a research design that deals with how many items are to be observed and how the data gathered are to be analysed is the statistical design. C. R. Kothari (1985), in Research Methodology: Methods and Techniques, a standard Indian textbook, splits a research design into four parts. The sampling design is the method of selecting the items to be observed. The observational design covers the conditions under which observations are made. The statistical design fixes how many items are to be observed (sample size) and how the data will be analysed. The operational design covers the techniques by which the procedures of the other three are actually carried out. The stem is Kothari's definition of statistical design almost word for word ('how many times items' is a slip for 'how many items'). Example: Riya plans a survey of screen time among Pune college students. Picking students by stratified random selection is her sampling design; collecting questionnaires in classrooms in the first week of term is her observational design; fixing 300 students and planning a t-test between men and women is her statistical design; training assistants to hand out the forms is her operational design. Option b is right because sample size and analysis are exactly what the statistical design settles. Exam trap: sampling design sounds as if it should cover 'how many', but in this four-part split it is the method of selecting items, while the number of items and the analysis belong to the statistical design.", "traps": {"a": "In Kothari's scheme the sampling design is the method of selecting the items to be observed (random, stratified, cluster and so on); it does not cover how the data will be analysed.", "c": "The observational design relates to the conditions under which observations are made, such as setting, timing and tools; it says nothing about sample size or analysis.", "d": "The operational design deals with the techniques by which the procedures of the sampling, observational and statistical designs are carried out in practice; it puts the plan into action rather than fixing numbers or analysis."}}, {"q": "A data set has a positive skewness when,", "concept": "The answer is b: a data set has positive skewness when mode < median < mean. Skewness is a lack of symmetry in a distribution, and it is named after its long tail. In a positive (right) skew most scores bunch at the low end and a few very high scores trail off to the right; incomes and the marks on a very hard test are classic cases. The three averages react differently to that tail. The mode, the most frequent score, stays at the peak of the hump. The median, the middle score, shifts a little towards the tail. The mean uses the value of every score, so the few extreme high scores drag it furthest to the right. Negative (left) skew is the mirror image, with a tail of low scores and mean < median < mode; in a symmetrical normal curve all three are equal. Karl Pearson (1895) measured skewness as the mean minus the mode, divided by the standard deviation, which comes out positive in a right skew. Example: in Arjun's village most families earn modest incomes, but two landowners earn crores, so the mean income is far above the median, and the median is above the most common income. Option b is right because in positive skew the mean is pulled up the most and the mode the least. Exam trap: option a is the order for negative skew, and students who only remember that the mean follows the tail can easily reverse it.", "traps": {"a": "Mean < median < mode is the order for a negatively (left) skewed distribution, where a tail of a few very low scores drags the mean down.", "c": "Mode = median = mean describes a symmetrical distribution such as the normal curve, which has zero skew.", "d": "Option b correctly states the order for positive skew, so 'none of the above' cannot be right."}}, {"q": "Fritz Heider gave which of the given theories of Attitude", "concept": "The answer is c: Fritz Heider (1946) gave the balance theory of attitudes. In 'Attitudes and cognitive organization' (1946), and more fully in The Psychology of Interpersonal Relations (1958), he argued that people want their attitudes and relationships to fit together harmoniously, which makes his a consistency theory. His model is the P-O-X triad: a person (P), another person (O) and an object, issue or third person (X), joined by positive relations (liking, agreeing) or negative ones (disliking). A triad is balanced when the product of the three signs is positive: all three positive, or two negative and one positive. It is imbalanced when the product is negative, and imbalance is felt as tension that pushes the person to restore balance by changing one of the relations, often an attitude. Example: Riya likes Kabir (+), Kabir loves cricket (+), but Riya dislikes cricket (-). The product is negative, so the triad is imbalanced. Riya may come to enjoy cricket, cool towards Kabir, or tell herself that he does not really care for the game. Heider also founded attribution theory, so his name turns up in two chapters of social psychology. Option c is right because balance theory, the P-O-X model, is Heider's contribution to attitude theory. Exam trap: cognitive dissonance theory is the best-known consistency theory and shares the idea of a pressure towards consistency, so it tempts; but it belongs to Leon Festinger (1957), not Heider.", "traps": {"a": "Cognitive dissonance theory is Leon Festinger's (1957): tension from holding inconsistent cognitions, shown in the one dollar versus twenty dollar study of Leon Festinger and James Carlsmith (1959); it is a consistency theory like Heider's, but not his.", "b": "Self-perception theory is Daryl Bem's (1967): people infer their own attitudes by watching their own behaviour, offered as a rival to dissonance theory, not Heider's work.", "d": "Persuasion is a broad research area, not one named theory; its classic models are the Yale communication research of Carl Hovland (Communication and Persuasion, 1953) and the elaboration likelihood model of Richard Petty and John Cacioppo (1986)."}}, {"q": "'Repeated exposure to a stimulus translates into enhanced liking for the stimulus' is stated by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the statement that repeated exposure to a stimulus raises liking for it is the mere exposure hypothesis, now usually called the mere exposure effect. Robert Zajonc (1968), in 'Attitudinal effects of mere exposure', presented nonsense words, Chinese-like characters and yearbook photographs of faces anywhere from 0 to 25 times, then asked people to guess how good the meaning of each word or character was, or how much they would like each man pictured. Liking rose steadily with the number of exposures, even though the items never changed and nothing pleasant was paired with them. In a striking follow-up, William Kunst-Wilson and Robert Zajonc (1980) flashed shapes too briefly to be recognised; people could not say which shapes they had seen, yet they preferred those shapes. So exposure can shape liking without conscious memory. One explanation is that familiar things are processed more easily, and that ease feels good and signals safety. The effect is largest for neutral stimuli and fades with very heavy repetition, when boredom sets in. Example: Kavya passes a new mural on her way to college every day. At first she finds it odd; a month later she likes it and is upset when it is painted over. Option a is right because the stem states exactly what Zajonc proposed: repetition alone increases liking. Exam trap: spatial proximity also increases liking, partly because people who live or sit near us are seen more often, so it is the near-miss; but proximity is about physical distance, and the stem names repeated exposure itself.", "traps": {"b": "The attractiveness stereotype is the belief that 'what is beautiful is good' (Karen Dion, Ellen Berscheid and Elaine Walster, 1972): good-looking people are assumed to have better traits; it is about looks, not repetition.", "c": "Spatial proximity (propinquity) is the finding that people who live or sit near each other become friends (Leon Festinger, Stanley Schachter and Kurt Back, 1950, Westgate study); it works partly through mere exposure, but it is about physical nearness.", "d": "Need complementarity is the idea of Robert Winch (1958) that people are drawn to partners whose needs complement their own (opposites attract), which says nothing about repeated exposure."}}, {"q": "When the likelihood to help in an emergency decreases because there are greater number of witnesses in an emergency, it is called", "concept": "The answer is b: when helping becomes less likely because more people are watching, the cause is called diffusion of responsibility. Each witness feels only a fraction of the duty to act and assumes that someone else will. John Darley and Bibb Latane (1968) set out to test this after the 1964 killing of Kitty Genovese in New York, which newspapers blamed on the apathy of 38 onlooking neighbours (a number later shown to be inflated). In their intercom experiment, a student in a cubicle heard a fellow participant seem to have a seizure. Of students who thought they were the only listener, about 85 percent went for help before the seizure ended; of those who thought four others were also listening, only about 31 percent did. This drop in helping with group size is the bystander effect, and diffusion of responsibility is its main mechanism. In the decision model of Bibb Latane and John Darley (1970), a helper must notice the event, interpret it as an emergency, take personal responsibility, know what to do and decide to act; diffusion knocks out the responsibility step. Example: Rohan sees a cyclist fall on a busy Bengaluru road, thinks 'With this crowd, someone must have called an ambulance,' and walks on. Option b is right because it names exactly the shared, thinned-out responsibility the stem describes. Exam trap: social influence (pluralistic ignorance: everyone looks calm, so each decides nothing is wrong) is the other bystander process; it affects how the event is interpreted, not who feels responsible.", "traps": {"a": "Social influence is the bystander process in which people look to others to decide whether an emergency is real, producing pluralistic ignorance (the smoke-filled room study of Bibb Latane and John Darley, 1968); it concerns interpreting the event, not the sharing of responsibility.", "c": "Negative state relief is the model of Robert Cialdini and colleagues (1973) that people help in order to lift their own sad mood; it explains why we help, not why more witnesses reduce helping.", "d": "Altruism is helping another out of concern for that person's welfare, with no expectation of reward; it is a motive for helping, not the name for the drop in help when witnesses increase."}}, {"q": "Who among the following developed an equation to compare mental age to chronological age which is popularly called Intelligence Quotient?", "concept": "The answer is c: William Stern (1912) proposed the equation that compares mental age with chronological age, the quotient later called the intelligence quotient. The story starts with Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905), who built the first practical intelligence scale in Paris to identify children who needed special schooling. In their 1908 revision items were arranged by the age at which typical children pass them, so a child's mental age (MA) was the highest age level he or she could manage. But a gap between mental and real age means different things at different ages: a 4-year-old working at the level of a 2-year-old is far more delayed than a 14-year-old working at the level of a 12-year-old. Stern, a psychologist in Germany, fixed this by dividing MA by chronological age (CA), giving a ratio that means the same at every age, and named it the Intelligenzquotient. Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied the ratio by 100, giving whole numbers, and printed it as IQ in the Stanford-Binet, the American revision that spread the term worldwide. Example: Arjun is 8 and passes the items typical of 10-year-olds, so his ratio IQ is 10 divided by 8, times 100, which is 125. Option c is right because Stern was the first to express intelligence as the ratio of mental to chronological age. Exam trap: Binet supplies the mental age and Terman the multiplication by 100 and the popular name, so both are tempting near-misses. Key note: many books credit Lewis Terman, because his 1916 Stanford-Binet put the IQ (MA divided by CA, times 100) into wide use, so b has some support; but the equation comparing mental age with chronological age is William Stern's, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Alfred Binet, with Theodore Simon, built the first intelligence scale (1905) and, in the 1908 revision, the idea of mental age, but he never turned mental age into a quotient.", "b": "Lewis Terman adapted Binet's scale as the Stanford-Binet (1916), multiplied Stern's quotient by 100 and popularised the term IQ, but the original equation was Stern's.", "d": "Theodore Simon was Binet's collaborator on the 1905 and 1908 scales; he shares credit for the first test and for mental age, not for the quotient."}}, {"q": "The tendency of people to behave differently on being knowingly observed is called:", "concept": "The answer is d: changing one's behaviour because one knows one is being watched is called the Hawthorne effect. The name comes from a long series of studies (1924 to 1932) at the Hawthorne Works, a Western Electric telephone equipment factory near Chicago. Engineers first varied the lighting to see whether brighter light raised output. Output went up, but it also went up in a comparison group whose lighting never changed, and in some trials it stayed up even when the lights were dimmed. Later, in the relay assembly test room, a small team of women kept producing more through almost every change in rest pauses and hours. The workers seemed to respond to the special attention of being studied rather than to the changes themselves. The work is linked with Elton Mayo (1933) and was written up by Fritz Roethlisberger and William Dickson (1939) in Management and the Worker; the label was coined by Henry Landsberger (1958). Modern reanalyses find the data weaker than the legend, but the effect remains a standard threat to the validity of research. Example: when a district inspector sits in on lessons at Meera's school, every teacher arrives early and puts up charts, then slips back into old habits once the visit is over. Option d is right because the stem describes a change caused by awareness of being observed. Exam trap: the placebo effect also rests on expectation, but its source is the belief that one is being treated, not the knowledge that one is being watched.", "traps": {"a": "The placebo effect is improvement that comes from believing one is receiving an effective treatment, even an inactive sugar pill; it depends on expectations about a treatment, not on being watched.", "b": "The halo effect, named by Edward Thorndike (1920), is a rating bias in which one good trait (looks, charm) colours judgements of a person's other traits; it is a bias in the observer, not a change in the person observed.", "c": "The horn effect is the negative halo: one bad trait makes the rater judge a person's other qualities unfavourably; again it is a bias in the rater, not a change in the behaviour of the person observed."}}, {"q": "Wechsler tests have:", "concept": "The answer is c: the classic Wechsler intelligence tests have both a verbal scale and a performance scale. David Wechsler (1939), chief psychologist at Bellevue Hospital in New York, found the Stanford-Binet poorly suited to adults: it leaned heavily on words and relied on mental age. His Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scale (1939) placed a Performance scale of non-verbal tasks beside the Verbal scale, and the Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (WISC, 1949) and Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS, 1955) kept the same plan. The Verbal subtests were information, comprehension, arithmetic, similarities, digit span and vocabulary; the Performance subtests were picture completion, picture arrangement, block design, object assembly and digit symbol. Each scale gave its own IQ, and together they gave a Full Scale IQ, all as deviation IQs (mean 100, SD 15). A large gap between Verbal and Performance IQ was read as a clinical clue. In India, Prabha Ramalingaswamy (1974) adapted the performance subtests for adults as the Wechsler Adult Performance Intelligence Scale (WAPIS). Example: Kabir, 30, who left school early, gets a Verbal IQ of 88 but a Performance IQ of 108, and his psychologist notes that limited schooling, not reasoning, is pulling the verbal score down. Option c is right because a verbal and a performance scale side by side is the defining design of the Wechsler tests. Exam trap: students who know that Wechsler added non-verbal tasks pick 'performance scale' alone. Key note: since the WISC-IV (2003) and WAIS-IV (2008), Verbal and Performance IQs have been replaced by four index scores (verbal comprehension, perceptual reasoning, working memory, processing speed); but the classic Wechsler scales had both, which is what this question means, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "A verbal scale is only half the design; Wechsler's innovation was to set a performance scale of non-verbal tasks beside it, so 'verbal scale' alone is incomplete.", "b": "The performance scale (block design, picture completion and so on) is the half Wechsler added to the older, word-heavy Stanford-Binet approach, but his tests kept a verbal scale as well.", "d": "The classic Wechsler tests had both a verbal and a performance scale, so 'none of the above' is false."}}, {"q": "_______ indicates how well a person performed on an IQ test relative to the particular age group the subject is a member of.", "concept": "The answer is b: a deviation IQ tells you how well a person did compared with people of his or her own age group. David Wechsler (1939) brought it in with the Wechsler-Bellevue scale. The test is first normed on a large sample at each age; a person's raw score is then expressed as a distance from his or her age group's mean, in standard deviation (SD) units, and converted to a scale with a mean of 100 and an SD of 15; the Stanford-Binet used an SD of 16 until its fifth edition (2003). So 115 is one SD above the age mean, better than about 84 percent of age-mates, and 130 is two SD above, about the 98th percentile. Because each score is tied to the person's own age group, it means the same at 8 or at 48. The ratio IQ of William Stern (1912) and Lewis Terman (1916), mental age over chronological age times 100, works differently: it compares a person's mental age with his or her own age, and it breaks down in adulthood, when mental age stops growing but birthdays continue. Example: Arjun, 12, scores 85 on the WISC, one SD below the mean for his age, so he did better than only about 16 percent of 12-year-olds in the norm group. Option b is right because only the deviation IQ is defined by comparison with one's own age group. Exam trap: the ratio IQ also involves age, so 'both' looks safe; but it compares two ages within one person, not a person with peers.", "traps": {"a": "The ratio IQ (William Stern's quotient, multiplied by 100 by Lewis Terman) compares a person's mental age with his or her own chronological age, not with the scores of the age group, and it breaks down in adults.", "c": "Only the deviation IQ compares a person with the distribution of scores in the same age group; the ratio IQ uses age in a different way, so 'both' is wrong.", "d": "The deviation IQ fits the definition exactly, so 'none of the above' is false."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a form of projective test", "concept": "The answer is d: the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) is not a projective test; it is an objective self-report inventory. A projective test gives an ambiguous stimulus and freedom to answer in almost any way, so the answer is assumed to reveal the person's needs, conflicts and unconscious wishes. Lawrence Frank (1939) coined the term projective methods for such tools. The Rorschach test of Hermann Rorschach (1921) uses ten inkblots; the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) of Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray (1935) asks for stories about vague pictures; and the Incomplete Sentences Blank of Julian Rotter and Janet Rafferty (1950) asks the person to finish 40 sentence stems. An objective inventory uses clear statements, fixed answer choices and keyed scoring. The MMPI, built by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943), has about 550 true or false statements scored on 10 clinical scales plus validity scales (L, F and K) that catch faking and careless answering. Its items were empirically keyed, chosen because patient groups answered them differently. Example: Kabir marks 'I wake up tired most mornings' as true and is compared with norms; Meera tells a story about a TAT picture and the psychologist reads her needs into it. Option d is right because the MMPI's fixed true or false format and norm-based scoring make it the classic objective test, the opposite of a projective technique. Exam trap: sentence completion looks structured, so students wrongly pick it; it is a completion technique, one of Gardner Lindzey's (1959) five families of projective methods; its stems add some structure, but it remains projective.", "traps": {"a": "The Rorschach inkblot test (Hermann Rorschach, 1921) is the best-known projective test: ten ambiguous inkblots onto which the person projects meaning, so it belongs to the group and cannot be the odd one out.", "b": "The Thematic Apperception Test (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935) asks for stories about ambiguous pictures that are read for the teller's needs; it is a projective construction technique, so it belongs.", "c": "The Rotter Incomplete Sentences Blank (Julian Rotter and Janet Rafferty, 1950) is a projective completion technique; its stems give some structure, which is why it is sometimes called semi-projective, but it is still classed as projective."}}, {"q": "\"Barnum effect\" refers to the:", "concept": "The answer is a: the Barnum effect is the tendency of people to accept, and approve of, a general personality interpretation as an accurate description of themselves. Such statements feel personal but fit almost everyone ('You have a great need for others to like you'). Bertram Forer (1949) demonstrated it in his class. He gave students a personality test, then handed each one the same 'individual' sketch, put together largely from an astrology book. Asked how well it described them, they rated it on average 4.26 out of 5. Paul Meehl (1956) gave the effect its name, after the showman P. T. Barnum, whose shows were said to have a little something for everybody; it is also called the Forer effect. Acceptance rises when the sketch is flattering, seems prepared for the person alone and comes from an authority; hence the appeal of horoscopes and palm readers. In assessment, a client nodding along to a report is no proof that the report is valid. Example: Riya reads her weekly horoscope, 'you are independent but sometimes doubt your own decisions', and feels it was written for her, though it fits each of her friends just as well. Option a is right because the Barnum effect is, by definition, uncritical acceptance of a general interpretation as one's own. Exam trap: examiners reverse the definition (rejecting the interpretation) or offer a cautious middle path (staying neutral, seeking more information); the effect is about believing too readily, not doubting.", "traps": {"b": "Rejecting and disapproving of a personality interpretation is the reverse of the Barnum effect; Forer's students did the opposite, and no named effect describes this.", "c": "Staying neutral about a personality interpretation is not a named effect; it is a distractor made by softening the real definition.", "d": "Seeking more information before judging is careful, critical behaviour, which is exactly what the Barnum effect shows people tend to skip; it is not a named effect."}}, {"q": "Which neurological disorder is characterized by the degeneration of dopamine producing neurons in the substantia nigra?", "concept": "The answer is b: Parkinson's disease is the neurological disorder in which the dopamine-producing neurons of the substantia nigra degenerate. The substantia nigra ('black substance', dark with the pigment neuromelanin) is a nucleus in the midbrain. Its pars compacta sends dopamine along the nigrostriatal pathway to the striatum (caudate nucleus and putamen), part of the basal ganglia, which help to start movements and keep them smooth. James Parkinson (1817) described the illness as the 'shaking palsy'. Signs usually appear only after roughly 60 to 80 percent of the nigral dopamine cells have been lost: a resting tremor (often 'pill-rolling'), rigidity, bradykinesia (slowness of movement), a shuffling gait, a mask-like face and poor balance. Oleh Hornykiewicz (1960) found that patients' striatum was almost empty of dopamine, and the surviving nigral cells contain Lewy bodies, clumps of the protein alpha-synuclein. Treatment uses L-dopa, a precursor that, unlike dopamine itself, crosses the blood-brain barrier. Example: Kabir notices that his grandmother's thumb and fingers rub together, as if rolling a pill, while she watches television, that her face has become expressionless, and that she needs several tries to rise from a chair; these point to Parkinson's disease. Option b is right because the loss of nigral dopamine neurons is the defining pathology of Parkinson's disease. Exam trap: students reverse the dopamine story. Too little dopamine in the nigrostriatal pathway gives Parkinson's disease, while too much dopamine activity in the mesolimbic pathway is linked with schizophrenia. Alzheimer's disease, the other famous neurodegenerative illness, is tied to acetylcholine loss, not nigral dopamine.", "traps": {"a": "Alzheimer's disease (Alois Alzheimer, 1906) is a dementia marked by amyloid plaques, tau tangles and loss of acetylcholine neurons in the basal forebrain, with memory loss first; the substantia nigra is not its target.", "c": "Multiple sclerosis is an autoimmune disease that strips myelin from axons in the brain and spinal cord, giving patchy visual, sensory and motor symptoms; it does not destroy dopamine neurons.", "d": "'None of these' fails because Parkinson's disease, option b, fits the description exactly."}}, {"q": "Rajat is experiencing sudden episodes of intense dread along with shortness of breath, heart palpitations which come and go. He is suffering from:", "concept": "The answer is c: Rajat's sudden episodes of intense dread with shortness of breath and palpitations, which come and go, describe panic disorder. In DSM-5 (2013) a panic attack is an abrupt surge of intense fear or discomfort that peaks within minutes and brings at least 4 of 13 symptoms: pounding heart, sweating, trembling, shortness of breath, a choking feeling, chest pain, nausea, dizziness, chills or heat, numbness or tingling, feelings of unreality, fear of losing control or 'going crazy', and fear of dying. Panic disorder is diagnosed when unexpected attacks recur, coming 'out of the blue', and at least one attack is followed by a month or more of persistent worry about further attacks or a maladaptive change in behaviour, such as avoiding exercise. David Clark (1986) explained panic as catastrophic misinterpretation of bodily sensations: a racing heart is read as a heart attack, fear rises, the heart races faster, and the spiral peaks in panic. CBT with interoceptive exposure (deliberately bringing on the feared sensations) and SSRIs are first-line treatments. Rajat's episodes are sudden, intense, brief and recurrent, with the classic bodily signs; nothing points to obsessions, trauma or constant worry. Option c is right because recurring, discrete surges of fear with bodily symptoms are the core of panic disorder. Exam trap: generalised anxiety disorder is the near miss, since it also involves anxiety and bodily tension; but GAD is steady, free-floating worry on most days for at least six months, not attacks that peak within minutes and pass.", "traps": {"a": "Generalised anxiety disorder is excessive, hard-to-control worry about many everyday matters on most days for at least six months, with tension and restlessness; it is continuous, not a series of sudden attacks.", "b": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves intrusive obsessions and repetitive compulsions performed to reduce the distress they cause; Rajat reports no obsessions or rituals.", "d": "Post-traumatic stress disorder follows a traumatic event and brings intrusive memories, avoidance and hyperarousal for more than a month; no trauma is mentioned for Rajat, and his attacks are not tied to reminders."}}, {"q": "Which of these is not a part of Cluster B personality disorders", "concept": "The answer is b: obsessive-compulsive personality disorder (OCPD) is not a Cluster B disorder; it belongs to Cluster C. A personality disorder is an enduring, inflexible pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates from cultural expectations, begins by early adulthood and causes distress or impairment. DSM-5 (2013) groups its ten specific personality disorders into three clusters by surface resemblance. Cluster A is odd or eccentric: paranoid, schizoid and schizotypal. Cluster B is dramatic, emotional or erratic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic and narcissistic. Cluster C is anxious or fearful: avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive. A simple memory aid is 'weird, wild, worried'. OCPD is a pervasive preoccupation with orderliness, perfectionism, rules and control, at the cost of flexibility, openness and efficiency. This tight, anxious over-control fits the fearful Cluster C, not the dramatic, attention-seeking style of Cluster B. Example: Meera spends weekends drawing up colour-coded schedules, insists the family load the dishwasher her way, and cannot finish her thesis because no chapter is ever perfect; that is the OCPD pattern, and it feels right to her. Option b is right because narcissistic, histrionic and borderline disorders all sit in Cluster B, and OCPD is the only one that does not, so it is the odd one out. Exam trap: do not confuse OCPD with obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). OCD has true obsessions and compulsions that the person usually experiences as unwanted (ego-dystonic), and DSM-5 places it in its own chapter of obsessive-compulsive and related disorders. OCPD has no true obsessions or rituals, and its traits feel right to the person (ego-syntonic).", "traps": {"a": "Narcissistic personality disorder (grandiosity, a need for admiration, lack of empathy) is a dramatic disorder and does belong to Cluster B, so it cannot be the exception.", "c": "Histrionic personality disorder (excessive emotionality, attention seeking, shallow and shifting emotions) is a core Cluster B disorder, so it belongs.", "d": "Borderline personality disorder (unstable relationships, self-image and mood, marked impulsivity, self-harm) is the best-known Cluster B disorder, so it belongs."}}, {"q": "Shashi witnessed brutal killings, and screams of children during war. After returning, he suffered from recurring haunting memories and nightmares, a numbed social withdrawal, jumpy anxiety, and insomnia. He may be experiencing:", "concept": "The answer is a: Shashi's symptoms after witnessing killings in war fit post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). Soldiers' breakdowns were once called shell shock, a term used by Charles Myers (1915), and later combat fatigue; PTSD entered the manuals only with DSM-III (1980), largely through work with Vietnam veterans. DSM-5 (2013) first requires exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence, by experiencing it, witnessing it or learning that it happened to someone close. Four symptom clusters follow. Intrusion: unwanted memories, nightmares, flashbacks, distress at reminders. Avoidance: of trauma-related thoughts, places and people. Negative changes in thinking and mood: guilt, blame, detachment from others, loss of interest and inability to feel positive emotions (emotional numbing). Changes in arousal and reactivity: irritability, hypervigilance, an exaggerated startle response, poor concentration and disturbed sleep. Symptoms must last more than one month; from 3 days to 1 month the diagnosis is acute stress disorder. Trauma-focused CBT, prolonged exposure (developed by Edna Foa) and eye movement desensitisation and reprocessing (EMDR; Francine Shapiro, 1989) are first-line treatments. Shashi meets every part: the trauma (witnessed killings), intrusion (haunting memories, nightmares), negative mood (numbed withdrawal) and arousal (jumpiness, insomnia). Option a is right because a qualifying trauma followed by intrusions, numbing and hyperarousal is the textbook picture of PTSD. Exam trap: generalised anxiety disorder also brings anxiety and poor sleep, and 'dissociative' sounds right for flashbacks; but GAD needs no trauma and has no re-experiencing, and flashbacks inside PTSD do not make it a separate dissociative disorder.", "traps": {"b": "Generalised anxiety disorder is chronic worry about many everyday matters for at least six months and needs no trauma; it cannot explain Shashi's nightmares and haunting memories of the war.", "c": "Dissociative disorders (dissociative amnesia, dissociative identity disorder, depersonalisation-derealisation) are breaks in memory, identity or awareness; Shashi has lost neither memory nor identity, he relives the trauma too vividly.", "d": "'None of the above' fails because PTSD, option a, accounts for every symptom in the stem."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a subtype of schizophrenia?", "concept": "The answer is d: catatonic, paranoid and undifferentiated are all classic subtypes of schizophrenia, so 'all of the above' is correct. Emil Kraepelin (1899) brought together three conditions described earlier, hebephrenia, catatonia and paranoid forms, as varieties of one illness, dementia praecox. Eugen Bleuler (1911) renamed it schizophrenia. DSM-IV (1994) and DSM-IV-TR (2000) listed five subtypes by the most prominent symptoms. Paranoid type: prominent delusions and voices, with thinking fairly intact. Disorganised type: disorganised speech and behaviour with flat or inappropriate affect. Catatonic type: motor signs such as stupor, posturing or excitement. Undifferentiated type: criteria met but no single picture dominates. Residual type: active symptoms gone, mild or negative symptoms remain. DSM-5 (2013) dropped all the subtypes because they were unreliable, unstable over time and did not predict treatment response, and made catatonia a specifier that can be added to schizophrenia and other disorders. Example: Kabir stands mute in one odd posture for hours and holds his arm wherever the nurse places it (waxy flexibility); under DSM-IV he would have been 'catatonic type', and under DSM-5 he has 'schizophrenia, with catatonia'. Option d is right because each of options a, b and c names a recognised classic subtype. Exam trap: students who know that DSM-5 abolished the subtypes may refuse every option; others pick the single most famous one (paranoid) and miss 'all of the above'. Key note: DSM-5 and ICD-11 no longer use subtypes, and 'catatonia' is now a specifier rather than the 'catatonic type'; but all three are the classic subtypes this question means, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Catatonia stands for the catatonic subtype (stupor, posturing, waxy flexibility or excitement); it is a genuine classic subtype, but choosing it alone ignores the other two.", "b": "The paranoid type (prominent delusions and voices with fairly intact thinking) was the commonest classic subtype; correct, but incomplete when the others also qualify.", "c": "The undifferentiated type (criteria met, but no one subtype's picture dominates) was also a DSM-IV and ICD-10 subtype, so picking it alone is incomplete."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a positive symptom of Schizophrenia?", "concept": "The answer is d: a toneless voice is not a positive symptom of schizophrenia; it is a negative symptom. Positive symptoms are excesses or distortions, something added to normal experience: hallucinations (usually voices), delusions, disorganised speech and grossly disorganised behaviour, including inappropriate affect such as laughing while describing a death. Negative symptoms are losses or reductions of normal function: affective flattening or diminished emotional expression (an unmoving face, a monotonous voice), alogia (poverty of speech), avolition (loss of goal-directed activity), anhedonia and asociality. Timothy Crow (1980) linked positive symptoms (Type I) with dopamine overactivity and good drug response, and negative symptoms (Type II) with structural brain changes. Nancy Andreasen rated them on separate scales, the SANS (1982) and the SAPS (1984). Example: Meera believes the television sends her secret messages, a positive symptom. Her mother notices that Meera now answers in a flat, unchanging tone and her face stays blank even at good news; that toneless speech is negative. Option d is right because a toneless voice reflects reduced emotional expression, a loss of normal function, which places it among the negative symptoms. Exam trap: inappropriate laughter and flat affect sound alike but fall on opposite sides. Inappropriate affect is emotion present but wrong for the situation; flat affect is emotion missing. Key note: some texts, following Peter Liddle (1987), list inappropriate affect under a third, disorganised group rather than with the positive symptoms; either way it is not a deficit, and a toneless voice is the classic negative symptom, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Hallucinations, perceptions without any external stimulus, are a core positive symptom, an addition to normal experience, so they cannot be the exception.", "b": "Disorganised speech and delusional talk are distortions of the form and content of thought, both standard positive symptoms, so this option belongs.", "c": "Inappropriate laughter is inappropriate affect: emotion present but out of keeping with the situation; it is counted with the positive (or disorganised) symptoms, not as a deficit, so it belongs."}}, {"q": "Which of these illnesses is related to abnormality in the dopaminergic system?", "concept": "The answer is d: both schizophrenia and Parkinson's disease are linked to abnormality in the dopaminergic system. Dopamine is a catecholamine neurotransmitter carried along several pathways. The nigrostriatal pathway runs from the substantia nigra to the striatum and controls movement; the mesolimbic pathway runs from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens and limbic areas and handles reward and salience. In Parkinson's disease the nigrostriatal neurons die, so there is too little dopamine, giving tremor, rigidity and slowness; L-dopa replaces it. In schizophrenia the dopamine hypothesis holds that too much dopamine activity in the mesolimbic pathway produces positive symptoms. Arvid Carlsson and Margit Lindqvist (1963) proposed that antipsychotic drugs work by blocking monoamine receptors, Philip Seeman and colleagues (1976) showed that their clinical potency tracks how strongly they bind dopamine receptors of the kind later named D2, and amphetamine, which releases dopamine, can cause a paranoid psychosis. The two illnesses are mirror images. Example: Meera's uncle, treated for schizophrenia with haloperidol, a strong dopamine blocker, develops stiff arms, a tremor and a shuffling walk (extrapyramidal side effects); in the other direction, too much L-dopa can bring on hallucinations in Parkinson's patients. Option d is right because Parkinson's disease involves too little dopamine and schizophrenia too much, so both are dopaminergic disorders and the option naming both is the complete answer. Exam trap: options a and b are each true but incomplete; Alzheimer's disease is the neurodegenerative lure, but its core transmitter loss is acetylcholine.", "traps": {"a": "Schizophrenia is linked to mesolimbic dopamine overactivity, so this is true, but it leaves out Parkinson's disease, which option d includes.", "b": "Parkinson's disease is caused by loss of nigrostriatal dopamine neurons, so this is true, but it leaves out schizophrenia and is incomplete.", "c": "Alzheimer's disease is mainly a cholinergic disorder (loss of acetylcholine neurons in the basal forebrain, with amyloid plaques and tau tangles), not a dopaminergic one."}}, {"q": "A person who is impatient, compulsive and competitive is likely to be diagnosed as having", "concept": "The answer is b: a person who is impatient, compulsive and competitive fits the Type A personality, or more exactly the Type A behaviour pattern. The idea came from two San Francisco cardiologists, Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman (1959), who noticed that many of their heart patients were driven, hurried people. They described Type A as intense competitive striving for achievement, a chronic sense of time urgency (impatience, doing several things at once, irritation at any delay), easily roused hostility and over-commitment to work. Type B people are relaxed, patient and easy-going. In their Western Collaborative Group Study of about 3,000 working men, followed through the 1960s, Type A men developed coronary heart disease about twice as often as Type B men. Later studies found that hostility, especially cynical hostility, is the harmful core, while time urgency on its own matters less. Example: Rohan finishes other people's sentences, drums his fingers in every queue, checks email at red lights and treats an office cricket match as a battle; he shows the Type A pattern. Option b is right because impatience (time urgency), competitiveness and a driven, compulsive work style are the textbook marks of Type A. Exam trap: the word 'compulsive' pulls students towards obsessive-compulsive disorder; but OCD is about obsessions and rituals, and 'compulsive' here means driven and unable to slow down. Key note: Type A is a behaviour pattern, not a diagnosis in DSM or ICD, so 'diagnosed' in the stem is loose; it still names the pattern described, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves intrusive obsessions and repetitive rituals done to reduce anxiety; it says nothing about competitiveness or time urgency.", "c": "Type B is the relaxed, patient, easy-going pattern that Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman contrasted with Type A, the opposite of the person described.", "d": "Machiavellianism (Richard Christie and Florence Geis, 1970) is a cynical, manipulative way of using others, part of the Dark Triad; it is not defined by impatience or time urgency."}}, {"q": "The most acceptable criterion of abnormality is the", "concept": "The answer is d: the statistical criterion, deviation from the average, is the classic and most widely cited single criterion of abnormality. It calls a behaviour or trait abnormal if it is rare, far from the population average. On the normal curve, scores in the extreme tails, say more than two standard deviations from the mean, count as deviant. Intellectual disability is partly defined this way: an IQ of about 70 or below lies two standard deviations under the mean of 100. It is objective and easy to measure. Its weakness is that rarity is not illness: a genius IQ is just as rare, and common problems such as depression and anxiety are not rare at all. Modern texts weigh it together with social norms, distress, dysfunction and danger (the 'four Ds': deviance, distress, dysfunction, danger). Example: Kabir sleeps only four hours a night, far less than almost everyone, so he is statistically deviant; yet if he feels fine and works well, few clinicians would call him disordered. Option d is right because, of the four options, only statistical deviation is a recognised criterion of abnormality. Exam trap: 'pathological' sounds medical and authoritative, but it is the very thing being defined, so it cannot serve as the defining criterion. Key note: no single criterion is accepted on its own, and modern texts put dysfunction and distress at the core; among these options, statistical deviation is the only real criterion and is the answer the official Gurugram key gives for the same question, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Personality is the pattern of traits that everyone has; it is what may be judged abnormal, not a yardstick for judging abnormality.", "b": "'Pathological' simply means diseased or abnormal, so it restates the thing to be defined; it is circular rather than a criterion.", "c": "Sociability is a single trait, close to extraversion; low sociability is common and normal, so it cannot define abnormality."}}, {"q": "\"A patient was highly disturbed by a morbid fear of travelling in a train, but he did not know why.\" Which type of disease the patient is suffering from?", "concept": "The answer is b: a morbid fear of travelling by train, whose cause the patient cannot explain, is a phobia, and in the older classification a phobia is a psychoneurosis. In the 1890s Sigmund Freud used the term psychoneurosis for non-psychotic disorders that he traced to unconscious conflict. Older systems, including DSM-I (1952) and DSM-II (1968), separated the psychoneuroses from the psychoses. Psychoneuroses bring marked distress, but the person stays in contact with reality, usually knows the fear is unreasonable and keeps his personality largely intact; examples were anxiety, phobic and obsessive-compulsive neuroses, hysteria and neurasthenia. Psychoses involve loss of contact with reality and often no insight. Freud explained phobias as anxiety displaced onto a symbolic object, as in his case of Little Hans (1909), whose fear of horses he traced to fear of the father; 'he did not know why' echoes that idea of an unconscious source. Example: Vikram takes long bus routes to avoid trains and admits the fear is silly but cannot explain it; he is distressed yet in touch with reality. Option b is right because an irrational, distressing fear with intact reality contact is the classic picture of a psychoneurosis (phobic neurosis). Exam trap: 'morbid' and 'highly disturbed' tempt students towards psychosis, but severity of distress does not make a disorder psychotic; only loss of reality contact does. Key note: DSM-III (1980) dropped the word neurosis, and today this would be a specific phobia (situational type) within the anxiety disorders; but the stem uses the old classification, in which a phobia is a psychoneurosis, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "A psychosomatic disorder is a real bodily illness, such as peptic ulcer, asthma or hypertension, caused or worsened by psychological factors (Franz Alexander, 1950); the patient has a fear, not a physical disease.", "c": "Psychosis means loss of contact with reality, with delusions, hallucinations and usually poor insight; the patient's fear is irrational, but he is in touch with reality.", "d": "Fatigue is a symptom, not a class of disease; the old 'fatigue neurosis', neurasthenia (George Beard, 1869), was itself classed among the neuroses, and the stem describes fear, not exhaustion."}}, {"q": "Which technique is none other than a de-conditioning or counter conditioning procedure used to breakdown neurotic anxiety reaction in the model of experimental neurosis", "concept": "The answer is c: systematic desensitisation is the deconditioning, or counterconditioning, procedure that grew out of breaking down neurotic anxiety in animals with experimental neurosis. In Ivan Pavlov's laboratory in the 1920s, dogs forced to tell a circle from an ever more circular ellipse broke down. In the late 1940s Joseph Wolpe gave cats electric shocks in a small cage until they were too anxious to eat there even when hungry. He then fed them in a room very unlike the cage, then in rooms more and more like it, and finally in the cage itself, until the anxiety was gone. Eating, incompatible with anxiety, had replaced the fear: counterconditioning. Wolpe called the principle reciprocal inhibition, borrowing the term from Charles Sherrington (1906): two incompatible responses cannot occur together. In Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958) he applied it to people, with deep muscle relaxation (from Edmund Jacobson, 1929) as the response that inhibits anxiety. The method has three steps: relaxation training, an anxiety hierarchy rated in subjective units of disturbance (SUDS), and graded exposure, usually imagined, moving up only when each step is calm. Example: Arjun, afraid of injections, relaxes while imagining a syringe in a picture, then a nurse preparing one, then the needle touching his arm. Option c is right because systematic desensitisation is precisely Wolpe's counterconditioning method, derived from his cure of experimental neurosis in cats. Exam trap: flooding and exposure with response prevention also treat anxiety, but they work through extinction and habituation without a competing response; counterconditioning by reciprocal inhibition is Wolpe's systematic desensitisation.", "traps": {"a": "'Psychotherapy' is the umbrella term for any psychological treatment; it names no specific deconditioning technique.", "b": "Non-directive therapy (Carl Rogers, 1942) is client-centred talk therapy relying on empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence; it uses no conditioning procedure.", "d": "Milieu therapy organises the whole ward as a therapeutic community, as in the work of Maxwell Jones in Britain in the 1940s and 1950s; it changes the social environment rather than counterconditioning a fear."}}, {"q": "Nowadays biofeedback is being used in the treatment of", "concept": "The answer is d: biofeedback is used today mainly for psychosomatic (psychophysiological) disorders and, as EEG neurofeedback, for epilepsy. In biofeedback a sensor measures a body process that a person normally cannot sense, such as muscle tension, skin temperature or brain waves, and displays it as a tone or graph. The person learns by trial and error to move the reading in a healthy direction, each success reinforced by the signal. The idea rests on Neal Miller (1969), who reported that autonomic responses, long thought to be involuntary, could be operantly conditioned in rats; the rat results later proved hard to replicate. Electromyographic (EMG) feedback of forehead muscle tension treats tension headache; thermal feedback, learning to warm the hands, treats migraine and Raynaud's disease. Barry Sterman found in the late 1960s that cats trained to produce a sensorimotor rhythm resisted drug-induced seizures, and EEG neurofeedback was then applied to people with epilepsy. Example: Meera has migraines. With a sensor on her finger, she watches a display of her skin temperature and learns, through calm imagery, to warm her hands, and her attacks grow rarer. Option d is right because psychosomatic disorders and epilepsy are the classic, best-supported uses of biofeedback. Exam trap: phobia tempts students because biofeedback teaches relaxation, but phobias are treated mainly by exposure and systematic desensitisation. Key note: biofeedback-assisted relaxation is also used as an aid in anxiety and phobias, so c has some support; but the classic textbook uses are psychosomatic disorders and epilepsy, which matches the official Gurugram answer, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Paranoia (delusional disorder, persecutory type) is a psychotic belief problem treated with antipsychotics and psychotherapy; learning to control a bodily signal cannot correct a delusion.", "b": "Catatonia is a motor syndrome (stupor, posturing, mutism) seen in schizophrenia, mood disorders and medical illness, treated with benzodiazepines such as lorazepam or with ECT, not biofeedback.", "c": "Phobias are treated mainly by exposure and systematic desensitisation; biofeedback-assisted relaxation is at most an aid there, not one of its classic uses."}}, {"q": "Psychotherapy in which the therapist prescribes remedies and courses of action as much as a physician prescribes medicine, used extensively in the early history of psychotherapy is known as", "concept": "The answer is b: directive therapy is psychotherapy in which the therapist takes charge, diagnoses the problem and prescribes remedies and courses of action, much as a physician prescribes medicine. The therapist is the expert: he gathers the facts, decides what is wrong and tells the client what to do, using advice, persuasion, suggestion and reassurance. This was the dominant style in the early history of psychotherapy: hypnosis and suggestion in the late nineteenth century, and the rational 'persuasion therapy' of the Swiss physician Paul Dubois (1904). The label became common after Carl Rogers (1942), in Counseling and Psychotherapy, called his own method non-directive, because his counsellor does not advise or steer. Directive counselling is a related but narrower idea: Edmund G. Williamson (1939, How to Counsel Students) set out a counsellor-centred method for students' educational and vocational problems in six steps: analysis, synthesis, diagnosis, prognosis, counselling and follow-up. Example: Meera says she cannot sleep before exams; her therapist diagnoses exam anxiety and prescribes a fixed bedtime, a revision timetable and breathing exercises. Option b is right because the stem describes a form of psychotherapy, therapist-led and prescriptive like medicine, that dominated its early history: directive therapy. Exam trap: directive counselling (option c) shares the word and the leading role, but it is Williamson's guidance method for normal students, not the early psychotherapy the stem describes. Key note: Anukriti's key table gives b, but her worked solution names c (directive counselling); the two ideas are close, but the stem asks for a psychotherapy, and directive counselling is Williamson's school and vocational guidance approach, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "'Psychotherapy' is the umbrella term for all psychological treatment; the stem asks for one particular kind of it, so the general word is too broad.", "c": "Directive (counsellor-centred) counselling is Edmund G. Williamson's (1939) six-step method for students' educational and vocational problems; it is guidance counselling, not the early psychotherapy described.", "d": "Non-directive therapy (Carl Rogers, 1942) is the opposite: the client leads, and the therapist offers empathy and acceptance instead of advice or prescriptions."}}, {"q": "Which psychotherapy was developed by Perls that attempts to restore a person's sense of wholeness until the individual becomes strong enough for growth to take place?", "concept": "The answer is a: Gestalt therapy, developed by Fritz Perls (1951), aims to restore a person's sense of wholeness so that growth can take place. Perls, a German-born psychoanalyst, built the approach with his wife Laura Perls and the writer Paul Goodman; the founding book, Gestalt Therapy (1951), was by Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Goodman. The German word Gestalt means a whole or complete form. Perls held that people become fragmented: they disown parts of themselves, carry 'unfinished business' from the past, and live in their heads instead of in present experience. Therapy aims at awareness and integration: reowning the split-off parts until the person is whole and self-supporting enough to mature. It stays in the here and now. The therapist asks 'what' and 'how' rather than 'why', and asks clients to take responsibility ('I won't' instead of 'I can't'). Its best-known technique is the empty chair: the client speaks to an absent person, or to a part of the self, then switches chairs to answer. Example: Riya still resents her elder sister for leaving home without a goodbye. In the empty chair she tells her sister how abandoned she felt, then answers as the sister, and the anger she had disowned becomes hers again. Option a is right because Gestalt therapy is Perls's, and its aim is integration of the fragmented self so that growth can follow. Exam trap: Gestalt therapy is not Gestalt psychology, the school of perception founded by Max Wertheimer (1912) with Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka; Perls only borrowed its idea of the whole.", "traps": {"b": "Yoga therapy draws on the Indian yoga tradition of postures, breathing and meditation (codified in Patanjali's Yoga Sutras); it has nothing to do with Perls.", "c": "Family therapy treats the family as a system, as in the structural approach of Salvador Minuchin (1974); it is not Perls's method.", "d": "Psychoanalytic therapy, developed by Sigmund Freud from the 1890s, uses free association and interpretation aimed at insight into the unconscious past; Perls trained in it but broke away to work on present awareness."}}, {"q": "A state of apprehension, tension and worry along with fear is characterized as", "concept": "The answer is a: a state of apprehension, tension and worry, accompanied by fear, is anxiety. Anxiety is a future-oriented emotion: the sense that something bad may happen, felt as worry in the mind, tension and arousal in the body. DSM-5 (2013) separates it from fear. Fear is the alarm reaction to a definite danger that is present now, such as a snake on the path; anxiety is apprehension about a possible future threat, such as worrying about snakes on tomorrow's trek. Anxiety has three parts: cognitive (worry, 'what if' thoughts), physiological (racing heart, muscle tension, sweating, poor sleep) and behavioural (avoidance, checking, reassurance seeking). In moderate amounts it is normal and useful; the law of Robert Yerkes and John Dodson (1908) shows that moderate arousal helps performance. It becomes a disorder when it is out of proportion to the danger, persistent and impairing. Charles Spielberger's State-Trait Anxiety Inventory (1970) separates state anxiety, how anxious one feels right now, from trait anxiety, a lasting tendency to feel anxious. Example: Arjun's job interview is a month away. Each night he lies awake picturing every question going wrong, his shoulders ache and his stomach churns at breakfast, though nothing bad has happened yet; that is anxiety. Option a is right because apprehension, tension and worry together form the definition of anxiety, with fear as its emotional colour. Exam trap: phobia also contains fear, so it lures students; but a phobia is fear tied to a specific object or situation, whereas the stem describes a general state without any named object.", "traps": {"b": "Depression is a state of low mood, loss of interest or pleasure, hopelessness and low energy; its core is sadness and loss, not apprehension about a threat.", "c": "A phobia is an intense, irrational fear of a specific object or situation, with avoidance; the stem describes a general state of worry with no particular object.", "d": "Schizophrenia is a psychotic disorder of delusions, hallucinations and disorganised thinking; it is not defined by worry and tension."}}, {"q": "Specific phobias are defined as:", "concept": "The answer is c: specific phobias are defined as an excessive, unreasonable and persistent fear triggered by a specific object or situation. In DSM-5 (2013) a specific phobia is marked fear or anxiety about a particular object or situation; the cue almost always provokes immediate fear; it is actively avoided or endured with intense distress; the fear is out of proportion to the actual danger; and it lasts typically six months or more, causing distress or impairment. DSM-5 lists five types: animal (dogs, spiders, snakes), natural environment (heights, storms, water), blood-injection-injury, situational (lifts, flying, enclosed spaces) and other (choking, vomiting). The blood-injection-injury type is unusual: heart rate and blood pressure rise and then fall sharply, so the person may faint. DSM-IV (1994) described the fear as 'excessive or unreasonable' and required the person to recognise this; DSM-5 dropped the insight rule and lets the clinician judge proportion, but 'unreasonable' and 'irrational' survive in exam wording. Example: Ananya has been terrified of dogs since one chased her at six. She crosses the road whenever she sees one, even a puppy on a leash, though she knows it cannot hurt her; that is an animal-type specific phobia. Option c is right because it states every defining feature: a fear that is excessive, unreasonable and persistent, cued by one specific object or situation. Exam trap: option d also describes a phobia, but fear of social situations and scrutiny is social anxiety disorder (social phobia), a separate DSM-5 diagnosis; and option a swaps fear for worry, the mark of generalised anxiety disorder.", "traps": {"a": "Bouts of worry are the hallmark of generalised anxiety disorder, which is apprehensive thinking about many matters; a phobia is immediate fear cued by an object, not worry.", "b": "Abnormal sensitivity to light is photophobia, a medical symptom of migraine, meningitis or eye inflammation; despite its name it is not an anxiety disorder.", "d": "A persistent fear of social situations, of being watched and judged, is social anxiety disorder (social phobia), a separate DSM-5 diagnosis rather than a specific phobia."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a predominant evolutionary theory of phobias?", "concept": "The answer is c: biological preparedness is the predominant evolutionary theory of phobias. Martin Seligman (1971) argued that evolution has prepared humans to learn some fears far more easily than others. Fears of snakes, spiders, heights, darkness and deep water protected our ancestors, so brains that learned them quickly had a survival advantage. Seligman built on John Garcia and Robert Koelling's (1966) finding that rats readily link a taste, but not a light or sound, with sickness. Prepared fears are learned fast, often in one trial, resist extinction, feel irrational and cluster around ancestral dangers. In the 1970s Arne Ohman and colleagues found that fear conditioned to snake and spider pictures outlasts fear conditioned to flowers or mushrooms. Michael Cook and Susan Mineka (1989) found that laboratory-reared monkeys learned fear of toy snakes, but not of flowers, by watching videos of frightened monkeys. Example: after one large spider drops onto Kabir's pillow, he cannot sleep in any room until it has been checked, yet he plugs in a frayed phone charger without a second thought. Option c is right because preparedness is the classic evolutionary account of why phobias cluster around ancestral dangers. Exam trap: option b is the tempting phrase, but plain conditioning has no evolutionary content: it says how a fear is acquired, not why the feared objects are so often spiders and heights rather than sockets and cars. Key note: the non-associative model (Richie Poulton and Ross Menzies, 2002) is also evolutionary, holding that some fears are innate and appear without any conditioning, so a has a case; but preparedness is the classic, predominant evolutionary account, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Non-associative fear acquisition (Richie Poulton and Ross Menzies, 2002) says some fears, such as of heights and water, are innate and need no learning; it is evolutionary too, but a newer minority view, not the predominant theory.", "b": "Learned fear responses describe plain conditioning, as in Little Albert (John Watson and Rosalie Rayner, 1920) or the two-factor theory of O. Hobart Mowrer (1947); this account makes no evolutionary claim.", "d": "'Specific phobia acquisition' is not a named theory; it simply restates the topic and is there only because it sounds technical."}}, {"q": "It is considered that successful CBT treatments of social phobia include elements of", "concept": "The answer is d: successful CBT for social phobia combines exposure, social skills training and cognitive restructuring, so 'all of the above' is correct. Social anxiety disorder (social phobia) is intense fear of situations in which one may be watched and judged. In the cognitive model of David Clark and Adrian Wells (1995), the person enters a social situation expecting to be judged, turns attention inward to monitor a negative self-image ('I'm visibly shaking'), and uses safety behaviours (avoiding eye contact, rehearsing sentences) that prevent him from learning his fears are exaggerated. Treatment attacks each link. Exposure means graded, repeated entry into feared situations, set up as behavioural experiments with safety behaviours dropped. Cognitive restructuring identifies and tests distorted thoughts such as 'everyone will notice I'm nervous and think I'm stupid'. Social skills training, through modelling, role-play and feedback, helps where real skill deficits keep the problem going. Richard Heimberg's cognitive behavioural group therapy, tested against a credible placebo in 1990, delivers exposure and cognitive restructuring in groups. Example: Kabir dreads speaking in seminars. He challenges the thought 'I will go blank and everyone will laugh', rehearses with his therapist, then presents to a small group without his script, and on video sees he looked calmer than he felt. Option d is right because each listed element targets a different maintaining factor, and effective programmes use them together. Exam trap: students who know that exposure is the most powerful ingredient pick option a, but the question asks what successful programmes include, and they include all three.", "traps": {"a": "Exposure therapy, graded and repeated facing of feared social situations, is a core ingredient, but on its own it leaves out the other two elements that the programmes also include.", "b": "Social skills training (modelling, role-play and feedback for conversation, assertiveness and eye contact) is part of the package, used where real deficits exist, but it is not the whole of it.", "c": "Cognitive restructuring, testing distorted thoughts about being judged, is central to the Clark and Wells approach, but choosing it alone ignores exposure and skills training."}}]});
